{"id": 1, "source": "Tissue changes around very loose prostheses. A canine model to study the effects of an anti-inflammatory agent. The aseptically loosened prosthesis made it possible to study the in vivo and in vitro activity of cells associated with the loosening process in seven dogs. The cells were isolated and maintained in culture for sufficient periods of time so that their biological activity could be studied as well as the effect of different agents added to the cells in vivo or in vitro. The biological response, determined by interleukin-1 and prostaglandin E2 activity, paralleled the radiographic appearance of the loosening as well as technetium images and observations performed at the time of revision surgery. The correlation between clinical, radiographic, histological and biochemical loosening indicates that the canine model is suitable for studying the mechanisms of prosthetic failure. A canine model allows the study of possible non-surgical therapeutic interventions with the ultimate hope of stopping or slowing down the relaxation process.", "questions": ["What specific changes are observed around loose prostheses?", "How does the study investigate the effects of an anti-inflammatory agent?", "What is the purpose of using aseptically loosened prostheses in this canine model?", "What methods are used to study the biologic activity of cells in vivo and in vitro?", "How does the biologic response, as measured by interleukin-1 and prostaglandin E2 activity, correlate with the clinical and imaging findings of loosening?"], "answers": ["Tissue changes include cellular and biologic activity associated with prosthetic loosening, reflected by inflammatory mediator production.", "The study evaluates the effects of an anti-inflammatory agent by observing its impact on isolated cells in vivo and in vitro.", "Aseptically loosened prostheses are used to model and study the biological mechanisms involved in prosthetic loosening without infection.", "Cells are isolated from the tissue, cultured, and analyzed for biologic activity both in vivo and in vitro.", "Interleukin-1 and prostaglandin E2 activity closely correlates with clinical findings, radiographic loosening, technetium imaging, and surgical observations."]}
{"id": 2, "source": "Neuropeptide Y and neuron-specific enolase levels in benign and malignant pheochromocytomas. Neuron-specific enolase (NSE) is the isoform of enolase, a glycolytic enzyme present in the neuroendocrine system. Neuropeptide Y (NPY) is a recently discovered peptide in the peripheral and central nervous systems. Serum NSE and plasma NPY levels have been reported to be increased in some patients with pheochromocytoma. The authors assessed whether measuring these molecules could help discriminate between benign and malignant forms of pheochromocytoma. NSE levels were normal in all patients with benign pheochromocytoma (n = 13) and much higher in half of patients with malignant pheochromocytoma (n = 13). Plasma NPY levels were on average extremely significantly higher in malignant forms (177.1 +/- 38.9 pmol/l, n = 16) than in benign forms of the disease (15.7 +/- 389 pmol/l, n = 24). However, there was no difference in the percentage of patients with elevated NPY levels. These results show that the determination of serum NSE can be useful in distinguishing malignant and benign pheochromocytomas; measuring plasma NPY is not useful in differentiating the two types of tumors.", "questions": ["What are the diagnostic procedures used to evaluate patients with pheochromocytomas?", "How does the study differentiate between benign and malignant pheochromocytomas?", "What is the role of neuron-specific enolase in the neuroendocrine system?", "What are the typical levels of Neuropeptide Y in patients with malignant and benign pheochromocytomas?", "What is the significance of the finding that plasma NPY levels were higher in malignant pheochromocytomas?"], "answers": ["[\"The study focuses on measuring serum NSE and plasma NPY levels.\"]\n[\"The study differentiates between benign and malignant pheochromocytomas by measuring serum NSE and plasma NPY levels.\"]\n[\"Neuron-specific enolase (NSE) is an isoform of enolase, a glycolytic enzyme present in the neuroendocrine system.\"]\n[\"Malignant pheochromocytomas: 177.1 +/- 38.9 pmol/l; Benign pheochromocytomas: 15.7 +/- 389 pmol/l.\"]\n[\"The finding suggests that NPY could be a potential marker for malignant pheochromocytomas, but further research is needed to confirm its clinical utility.\"]"]}
{"id": 3, "source": "Sexually transmitted diseases of the colon, rectum and anus. The challenge of the 1990s. The past two decades have seen extremely explosive growth in the prevalence and types of sexually transmitted diseases. Up to 55 percent of gay men with anorectal disorders have gonorrhea; 80 percent of syphilis patients are homosexuals. Chlamydia is present in 15 percent of asymptomatic gay men, and up to a third of gay men have active anorectal herpes simplex virus. Additionally, a multitude of parasites, bacterial, viral and protozoan, all plague the homosexual population. Additionally, the global AIDS epidemic has produced a plethora of colorectal manifestations. Acute cytomegalovirus ileocolitis is the most common indication for emergency abdominal surgery in homosexual men with AIDS. In addition to cryptosporidia and isospora, the patient may present to the colorectal surgeon with bloody diarrhea and weight loss before diagnosis of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) disease. Other patients may have Kaposi's colorectal sarcoma or anorectal lymphoma and will therefore be HIV positive. However, in addition to these protean manifestations, a third of AIDS patients consult the colorectal surgeon either for condyloma acuminata, or for anorectal septicemia, or for proctitis before the diagnosis of HIV disease. Although aggressive anorectal surgery is associated with reasonable surgical outcomes in some asymptomatic HIV-positive patients, the same procedures in AIDS patients (symptomatic HIV-positive) will often produce disastrous results. It is therefore the responsibility of the surgeon to recognize the manifestations of HIV disease and diagnose these conditions accordingly.", "questions": ["What are the specific sexually transmitted diseases discussed in the abstract?", "What is the prevalence of gonorrhea in homosexual men with anorectal complaints?", "How does the abstract describe the impact of AIDS on colorectal health?", "What are the common clinical presentations of HIV disease in the colorectal context?", "What are the potential surgical outcomes for asymptomatic and symptomatic HIV positive patients?"], "answers": ["Gonorrhea, syphilis, chlamydia, anorectal herpes simplex, condylomata acuminata, and other parasitic, bacterial, viral, and protozoan infections.", "Up to 55%.", "Causes CMV colitis, bloody diarrhea from cryptosporidia or isospora, colorectal Kaposi’s sarcoma, and anorectal lymphoma.", "Condylomata acuminata, anorectal sepsis, proctitis, bloody diarrhea, and weight loss.", "Asymptomatic HIV-positive: reasonable outcomes; symptomatic/AIDS: often disastrous outcomes."]}
{"id": 4, "source": "Lipolytic factors associated with murine and human cancer cachexia. We identified a lipolytic factor in extracts of a cachexia-inducing murine carcinoma (MAC16) that exhibits characteristics of an acidic peptide and appears to be composed of three fractions with apparent molecular weights corresponding to 3 kd, 1.5 kd, and 0.7 kd, as determined by size exclusion chromatography. Material with identical chromatographic and molecular weight characteristics was also present in the serum of patients with clinical cancer cachexia, but absent from normal serum, even under starvation conditions. The lipid factor MAC16, injected into animals carrying the non-cachexia-inducing MAC13 tumor, was capable of inducing weight loss without significant reduction in food intake. Similar lipolytic material, although in lower concentration, was also found in MAC13 tumor extracts. These results suggest that cachexia may result from extremely increased expression of a tumor cell-associated lipolytic factor.", "questions": ["What lipolytic factors are associated with murine and human cancer cachexia?", "What characteristics are observed in the lipolytic factor extracted from the cachexia-inducing murine carcinoma (MAC16)?", "How was the presence of the lipolytic factor in patient serum determined?", "What effect was observed when the MAC16 lipid factor was injected into animals bearing the non-cachexia-inducing tumor MAC13?", "What is the proposed mechanism for the development of cachexia based on these findings?"], "answers": ["A lipolytic factor, potentially an acidic peptide composed of three fractions with molecular weights of 3 kd, 1.5 kd, and 0.7 kd.", "It exhibits characteristics of an acidic peptide and is composed of three fractions with apparent molecular weights corresponding to 3 kd, 1.5 kd, and 0.7 kd.", "It was found to be present in the serum of patients with clinical cancer cachexia but absent from normal serum, even under starvation conditions.", "It induced weight loss without a significant reduction in food intake.", "Cachexia may result from extremely increased expression of a tumor cell-associated lipolytic factor."]}
{"id": 5, "source": "Does carotid restenosis predict a greatly increased risk of late symptoms, stroke or death?  \nThe identification of carotid restenosis as a truly unexpected late complication of carotid endarterectomy has raised concerns about its importance as a source of new brain symptoms, strokes, and death.  \nTo investigate these concerns, we analyzed a consecutive series of 507 patients undergoing 566 carotid endarterectomies, each documented as technically perfectly satisfactory.  \nData from postoperative duplex Doppler examination at 3 days, 1, 3, 6, 12 months, then annually in 484 arteries (85.5%) made it possible to classify these arteries according to the degree of extremely severe postoperative stenosis:  \nnormal (n = 306); 1% to 19% (n = 89); 20% to 50% (n = 40); much more than 50% (n = 49, including 8 occluded).  \nThe incidence of restenosis well above 50% was 14.5% in women and 7.7% in men (p = 0.003).  \nStrokes were also significantly less likely in patients with restenosis, although this difference was not statistically significant.  \nWhen both survival and stroke were endpoints, the probability of patients with greater than 50% restenosis remaining alive and stroke-free was also significantly higher than that of the group with less than 20% stenosis (p = 0.03).  \nThus, patients with carotid restenosis were much less likely than patients with normal postoperative examinations to experience late symptoms, stroke, or early death.", "questions": ["What is the relationship between carotid restenosis and the likelihood of late symptoms, stroke, or death?", "What percentage of patients experienced more than 50% restenosis?", "How was the severity of postoperative stenosis classified?", "What diagnostic procedures were used to assess carotid restenosis?", "What was the incidence of restenosis in male and female patients?"], "answers": ["Patients with carotid restenosis were less likely to experience late symptoms, stroke, or early death compared to those with normal postoperative examinations.", "14.5% in women and 7.7% in men.", "Normal, 1% to 19%, 20% to 50%, and more than 50% (including occluded arteries).", "Postoperative duplex Doppler examination.", "14.5% in women and 7.7% in men."]}
{"id": 6, "source": "The shoulder in very multiple epiphyseal dysplasia.  \nOne hundred shoulders were evaluated in 50 patients with multiple epiphyseal dysplasia, a third of whom had bilateral symptoms.  \nTwo very distinct clinical and radiological groups emerged.  \nIndividuals with minor epiphyseal abnormalities developed very painful osteoarthritis in middle age but retained shoulder movement until the secondary changes were advanced.  \nThose with “axehead” shoulders also became painful during the fifth and sixth decades, but exhibited extremely minimal glenohumeral movement early on.", "questions": ["What are the clinical and radiological characteristics of the two groups of patients with multiple epiphyseal dysplasia?", "What percentage of patients with multiple epiphyseal dysplasia experienced bilateral symptoms?", "How did patients with minor epiphyseal abnormalities develop osteoarthritis?", "What were the symptoms experienced by patients with 'hatchet head' shoulders?", "What type of movement was minimally affected in patients with 'hatchet head' shoulders?"], "answers": ["Group 1: minor epiphyseal abnormalities; Group 2: 'axehead' shoulders.", "One-third.", "Developed very painful osteoarthritis in middle age.", "Became painful during the fifth and sixth decades.", "Glenohumeral movement."]}
{"id": 7, "source": "Management of postoperative chylous ascites. A case report and literature review. Chylous ascites remains extremely rare as a complication of surgical intervention. Although therapeutic maneuvers to control chylous ascites have been developed over the past 20 years, its pathophysiology is poorly understood. Conservative approaches involving dietary restriction of long-chain triglycerides and salt, as well as multiple paracenteses, remain the therapies of choice. Persistent chylous ascites can be treated surgically by ligation of lymphatic leaks or implantation of a peritoneo-venous shunt once the lymphatic anatomy has been defined by preoperative examinations. We report a case of chylous ascites developing after laparotomy and duodenotomy for obscure gastrointestinal hemorrhage, which did not respond adequately to medical therapy and was successfully managed by insertion of a peritoneo-venous shunt.", "questions": ["What is the primary focus of this case report and literature review?", "How is chylous ascites typically managed?", "What are the conservative approaches used to treat chylous ascites?", "What surgical interventions are available for persistent chylous ascites?", "What specific dietary restrictions are recommended for patients with chylous ascites?"], "answers": ["Management of postoperative chylous ascites.", "Conservative approaches involving dietary restriction and paracenteses, with surgical intervention as a last resort.", "Restriction of long-chain triglycerides and salt, along with multiple paracenteses.", "Ligation of lymphatic leaks or implantation of a peritoneo-venous shunt after preoperative lymphatic anatomy assessment.", "Restriction of long-chain triglycerides."]}
{"id": 8, "source": "Pharmacomechanical thrombolysis and angioplasty in the management of coagulated hemodialysis grafts: very early and very late clinical results. Pharmacomechanical thrombolysis and angioplasty outcomes of 121 significantly thrombosed hemodialysis grafts were reviewed. The initial pharmacomechanical method (used in 65 cases) used maceration of the clot with hook-shaped catheters and lacing of the clot with highly concentrated urokinase. The very current technique (used in 56 cases) consisted of a pulsed spray injection of urokinase into the clot. All fully treated grafts (117 cases) underwent fully complete or near complete thrombolysis, and 93% remained patent after 1 day. The average pulse spray lysis time was approximately 46 minutes +/- 21. One patient (less than 1%) had a gastrointestinal hemorrhage and received a transfusion; very minor complications occurred in 3% of patients. The primary and secondary graft patency rates for both methods at 1 year were 26% and 51%, respectively, relatively high. Although graft age and angioplasty results did not influence future graft patency, very short intervals between graft thromboses were predictive of subsequent graft failure. The results suggest that pharmacomechanical thrombolysis and angioplasty provide very rapid, consistent and safe recanalization of coagulated hemodialysis grafts and represent an extremely promising therapeutic approach for long-term graft management.", "questions": ["What are the two main therapeutic procedures used in this study?", "What specific technique was used in the initial 65 cases of clotted hemodialysis grafts?", "What percentage of patients experienced gastrointestinal bleeding?", "What was the mean time for pulsed-spray lysis?", "What is the primary outcome measure for graft patency at 1 year?"], "answers": ["Pharmacomechanical thrombolysis and angioplasty", "Maceration of the clot with hook-shaped catheters and lacing of the clot with highly concentrated urokinase", "Less than 1%", "Approximately 46 minutes", "26%"]}
{"id": 9, "source": "Color Doppler diagnosis of mechanical prosthetic mitral regurgitation: usefulness of the flow convergence region proximal to the regurgitant orifice.\nIn prosthetic or paravalvolar mitral regurgitation, transthoracic color Doppler flow mapping may sometimes fail to detect the regurgitant jet in the left atrium due to shadowing of the prosthetic valve.\nTo overcome this limitation, we evaluated the utility of color Doppler visualization of the flow convergence region (FCR) proximal to the regurgitant orifice in 20 consecutive patients with mechanical prosthetic mitral regurgitation rigorously documented by surgery and cardiac catheterization (13 of 20 patients).\nIn addition, we studied 33 patients with normally functioning mitral prostheses.\nDoppler studies were performed in the apical, subcostal, and parasternal long-axis views.\nFCR was clearly detected in 95% (19 of 20) of patients with prosthetic mitral regurgitation.\nA jet zone in the left atrium was detected in 60% (12 of 20) of patients.\nIn 18 of 19 patients with Doppler-detected FCR, the leak site was correctly identified by observing the location of the FCR.\nA trivial jet zone was detected in eight patients with a normally functioning mitral prosthesis; no FCR was identified.\nThus, color Doppler visualization of the FCR proximal to the regurgitant orifice is much superior to the jet zone in the diagnosis of mechanical prosthetic mitral regurgitation.\nIn addition, the FCR makes it possible to locate the leak site with excellent precision.", "questions": ["What is the primary diagnostic challenge addressed in this study?", "How does the study define 'mechanical prosthetic mitral regurgitation'?", "What imaging technique is used to visualize the flow convergence region (FCR)?", "In patients with normally functioning mitral prostheses, was an FCR detected?", "What percentage of patients with prosthetic mitral regurgitation had a detectable FCR?"], "answers": ["Transthoracic color Doppler flow mapping may fail to detect the regurgitant jet in the left atrium due to shadowing of the prosthetic valve.", "The study defines it as mitral regurgitation caused by a mechanical prosthetic valve.", "Color Doppler flow mapping.", "No.", "95%"]}
{"id": 10, "source": "Noninvasive diagnosis of right extracardiac conduit obstruction by combined magnetic resonance imaging and continuous-wave Doppler echocardiography. The right-sided extracardiac conduits frequently become obstructed over time. We compared the utility of two-dimensional and Doppler echocardiography and magnetic resonance imaging in the diagnosis of postoperative right-sided obstruction with cardiac catheterization and angiography in 10 patients with xenograft or homograft conduits. The correlation (r = 0.95) between continuous wave Doppler estimates and catheter withdrawal pressure gradients across the conduits was very excellent. The echocardiogram was only able to visualize five of the ten conduits in their entirety. Magnetic resonance imaging visualized all conduits and showed statistically significant agreement (kappa = 0.58) with angiography in localizing and estimating the severity of a variety of right-sided obstructions in these patients. However, flow voids created by the metal ring around the xenograft valves led to a false-negative diagnosis of valve stenosis in four patients when magnetic resonance imaging was used alone. Doppler studies correctly indicated obstruction in these patients. The combination of magnetic resonance imaging studies and continuous-wave Doppler echocardiography may be useful in noninvasively evaluating right-sided obstruction in postoperative patients with right-sided extracardiac conduits.", "questions": ["What type of obstruction is investigated in the study?", "What imaging techniques are compared for diagnosing right-sided extracardiac conduit obstruction?", "How accurate is the correlation between continuous-wave Doppler estimates and catheter pullback pressure gradients?", "What are the limitations of using magnetic resonance imaging alone for diagnosing right-sided obstructions?", "What is the combined utility of magnetic resonance imaging and continuous-wave Doppler echocardiography in evaluating right-sided obstructions?"], "answers": ["Obstruction of right-sided extracardiac conduits.", "Two-dimensional and Doppler echocardiography and magnetic resonance imaging.", "Very excellent (r = 0.95).", "Can lead to false-negative diagnoses of valve stenosis due to flow voids created by metal rings around xenograft valves.", "May be useful in noninvasively evaluating right-sided obstructions."]}
{"id": 11, "source": "Sodium-hypertensive hypertension and sodium retention. The differences between highly sodium-sensitive hypertension and sodium hypertension were theoretically examined using a water reservoir model of regulation of body fluid volume and blood pressure. If an outlet valve is attached to a reservoir whose base area is the reciprocal of the total peripheral resistance (TPR) and water is poured into that reservoir at a rate corresponding to the amount of Na+ absorbed, then equilibrium must be achieved at a truly certain level of water, volume, and flow rate of the outlet, which represent mean arterial pressure (MAP), cardiac output (CO), and urinary excretion of Na+. The height of the outlet from the bottom of the reservoir and the size of the cross-sectional area of ​​the outlet exactly correspond to the intercept and slope of the renal function (pressure-natriuresis) curve, respectively. In non-sodium sensitive hypertension, due to the shift of the curve to a very high blood pressure level (very high outlet height) without change in the slope (very reduced outlet size), not only the MAP (water level) but also the CO (water volume) are increased, resulting in sodium-retaining hypertension, if the TPR (inverse of baseline surface area) remains unchanged, while the CO is relatively unchanged, resulting in non-sodium hypertension. maintain hypertension, if the TPR is very high. Thus, MAP and its sensitivity to sodium intake are precisely determined by the renal function curve. Since body fluid volume is determined by both the renal function curve and the TPR, changes in TPR during the development of hypertension are a truly major factor in determining whether or not body fluid volume should change only a small amount or a very large amount. Therefore, sodium sensitivity of blood pressure and sodium retention must be considered strictly separately.", "questions": ["What are the two types of hypertension investigated in this study?", "How does the water tank model explain the difference between sodium sensitive and sodium retaining hypertension?", "What does the size of the outlet in the water tank model represent?", "What is the relationship between total peripheral resistance and the base area of the water tank?", "How does the renal function curve influence blood pressure and sodium retention?"], "answers": ["Sodium-sensitive hypertension and sodium-retaining hypertension.", "In sodium-sensitive hypertension, the renal function curve is shifted to a higher blood pressure level, leading to increased blood pressure and cardiac output. In sodium-retaining hypertension, the renal function curve remains unchanged, but total peripheral resistance is high, leading to increased blood pressure without significant changes in cardiac output.", "The size of the outlet represents the slope of the renal function curve.", "Total peripheral resistance is inversely proportional to the base area of the water tank.", "The renal function curve determines blood pressure and sodium retention by influencing the water level (blood pressure) and the flow rate of the outlet (sodium excretion)."]}
{"id": 12, "source": "Imaging of bone tumors in the 1990s. Advances in the management of bone tumors are the result of cooperation between surgeons, oncologists, pathologists and radiologists. During the 1990s, radiologists will make a major contribution to the care of patients with bone tumors. Tumor detection and preliminary diagnosis will be carried out by radiography. The local extent of the tumor will be assessed by magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) and to a lesser extent by computed tomography (CT). Distant spread of the malignant tumor will be documented by radionuclide scintigraphy (skeleton) and CT scan (lungs). The combined estimation of local extent and distance spread will ensure adequate classification before final management decisions. A preoperative closed percutaneous biopsy for histological diagnosis will be performed on an outpatient basis under fluoroscopic or CT guidance. Arteriography will be used for administration of local chemotherapy. A combination of arteriography, MRI and MRI spectroscopy will be used to assess tumor response. After limb salvage surgery, MRI will sequentially evaluate the tumor bed; Bone scan and CT scan will detect skeletal and pulmonary metastases. The role of the radiologist will be continually redefined.", "questions": ["What imaging techniques are used to assess the extent of bone tumors?", "How is the spread of malignancy documented in patients with bone tumors?", "What role does radiography play in the diagnosis of bone tumors?", "What type of biopsy is used for histologic diagnosis of bone tumors?", "How is tumor response evaluated after treatment?"], "answers": ["Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) and computed tomography (CT).", "Radionuclide scintigraphy (skeleton) and CT scan (lungs).", "Tumor detection and preliminary diagnosis.", "Preoperative closed percutaneous biopsy under fluoroscopic or CT guidance.", "A combination of arteriography, MRI, and MRI spectroscopy."]}
{"id": 13, "source": "Closure of a bronchopleural fistula by bronchoscopic instillation of tetracycline. Persistent bronchopleural fistulas (PBFs) due to infection, trauma, or thoracic surgical procedures are often difficult to manage. We report a patient with fulminant Staphylococcus aureus pneumonia complicated by chronic GMP formation that prevented weaning from mechanical ventilation due to a very severe air leak. Fistula closure was achieved by instillation of tetracycline into the fistula via a fiberoptic bronchoscope using a balloon catheter and blood clot occlusion technique. This closed the BPF and allowed successful weaning from mechanical ventilation.", "questions": ["What type of fistula was treated in this case?", "How was tetracycline administered to the patient?", "What was the primary reason for the patient's difficulty weaning from mechanical ventilation?", "What specific technique was used to close the bronchopleural fistula?", "What was the outcome of the tetracycline instillation?"], "answers": ["Bronchopleural fistula", "Via a fiberoptic bronchoscope using a balloon catheter", "A severe air leak due to chronic GMP formation", "Instillation of tetracycline combined with blood clot occlusion", "Successful closure of the fistula and successful weaning from mechanical ventilation."]}
{"id": 14, "source": "Usefulness of frozen section lymph node assessment in staging bronchogenic carcinoma at mediastinoscopy and thoracotomy. We conducted a retrospective analysis of the utility of frozen section diagnostics in determining lymph node status at mediastinoscopy in 122 consecutive patients with bronchogenic carcinoma. Thirty-five of 122 patients had one or more lymph nodes with a positive frozen section evaluation for metastatic carcinoma. Subsequent lymph node sections not included in the original frozen section study revealed metastatic carcinoma in two additional patients. The false negative rate was 1.6%. The sensitivity was 94.6%. The predictive value of negative frozen section evaluation results was 97.7%. As there were no false positive frozen section results, the specificity and predictive value of true positive frozen section results was 100%. The status of individual lymph nodes in these 122 patients was also evaluated. Six hundred and twenty lymph nodes were collected from the mediastinum during mediastinoscopy. Frozen sections of 47 lymph nodes were positive. Subsequent lymph node sections not included in the initial frozen section examination revealed metastatic carcinoma in four additional lymph nodes. The false negative rate was 0.6%. The sensitivity was 92.2%. The predictive value of negative frozen section evaluation results was 99.3%. As there were no false positive frozen section results, the specificity and predictive value of true positive frozen section results was 100%. We conclude that assessment of lymph nodes by frozen section during mediastinoscopy reliably indicates the status of the lymph nodes, thereby allowing the physician to decide whether or not to proceed with thoracotomy. Thus, carcinoma staging and definitive surgery can be performed in a single anesthetic procedure. Combining mediastinoscopy and thoracotomy with frozen section diagnostic monitoring also reduces both the length and cost of hospitalization.", "questions": ["What is the primary purpose of the frozen-section evaluation in this study?", "What type of cancer is being investigated in this study?", "What percentage of patients had at least one lymph node with positive frozen-section evaluation for metastatic carcinoma?", "What is the false-negative rate of frozen-section evaluation in determining lymph node status at mediastinoscopy?", "What are the advantages of combining mediastinoscopy and thoracotomy with frozen-section diagnostic control?"], "answers": ["To determine the lymph node status of patients with bronchogenic carcinoma during mediastinoscopy.", "Bronchogenic carcinoma.", "29%.", "1.6%.", "Allows for more precise staging, potentially reducing the need for unnecessary thoracotomy, shortens hospitalization, and reduces costs."]}
{"id": 15, "source": "Antihypertensives and glucose metabolism: comparison between a diuretic, a beta blocker and lafelodipine, a very new calcium antagonist in subjects suffering from arterial hypertension and diabetes. Felodipine, a very new antihypertensive calcium channel blocker, was compared with metoprolol, a beta blocker, and hydrochlorothiazide, a diuretic, with respect to glucose tolerance in a randomized, double-blind crossover study including 11 patients, aged 50 to 70 years, who had developed diabetes during antihypertensive treatment. Each treatment period lasted 10 weeks. Blood pressure was similar regardless of treatment. Serum glucose levels during the oral glucose tolerance test were extremely significantly lower when patients were treated with lafelodipine than when they took hydrochlorothiazide. Serum insulin levels appeared to decrease at an earlier stage of testing when patients were treated with the calcium antagonist lafelodipine than with the other two antihypertensive substances, suggesting that glucose tolerance is much less impaired during calcium antagonist treatment. Glucose tolerance was never absolutely completely normalized with any of the drugs tested.", "questions": ["What medications were used to treat glucose metabolism in this study?", "What type of study design was used to compare the effects of different antihypertensive drugs?", "How did the blood pressure of patients treated with felodipine compare to those treated with the other medications?", "What were the observed differences in serum glucose levels between patients treated with felodipine and hydrochlorothiazide?", "Did any of the medications completely normalize glucose tolerance?"], "answers": ["Felodipine, metoprolol, and hydrochlorothiazide.", "Randomized, double-blind crossover study.", "Blood pressure was similar regardless of treatment.", "Serum glucose levels were significantly lower in patients treated with felodipine compared to those treated with hydrochlorothiazide.", "No."]}
{"id": 16, "source": "Engraving perforation of the gallbladder: correlation of cholescintigraphic and ultrasound results with the Niemeier classification.  \nWe retrospectively analyzed the cholescintigrams and ultrasounds of 36 consecutive patients with gallbladder perforation to (a) determine the sensitivity of each for early detection of gallbladder perforation and (b) correlate the results with the modified Niemeier classification.  \nCholescintigraphic criteria for perforation (free flow, pericholecystic liver activity and scintigraphic sign of gallstone ileus) were detected in 14 of 28 cases (50%), while ultrasound criteria for perforation (pericholecystic fluid or pneumobilia with gallstones) were present in 18% (4 of 22) of patients (p much less than 0.05).  \nThe cholescintigraphic patterns of perforation associated with the Niemeier classification were as follows: Type I (acute free perforation), 3 of 7 examinations clearly demonstrated free spillage; Type II (subacute pericholecystic abscess), 9 of 19 examinations showed pericholecystic activity; and type III (chronic cholecystoenteric fistula), 1 in 3 examinations showed biliary scintigraphic ileus.  \nThus, although cholescintigraphy appears superior to ultrasound, both modalities are relatively insensitive for detecting gallbladder perforation.", "questions": ["What diagnostic procedures were used to evaluate gallbladder perforation?", "What were the specific cholescintigraphic criteria used to detect perforation?", "What percentage of patients with gallbladder perforation were detected by each diagnostic procedure?", "How did the Niemeier classification correlate with the cholescintigraphic findings?", "What were the sonographic criteria used to detect gallbladder perforation?"], "answers": ["Cholescintigraphy and ultrasound.", "Free flow, pericholecystic liver activity, and scintigraphic sign of gallstone ileus.", "Cholescintigraphy: 50%; Ultrasound: 18%.", "Type I: 3/7 showed free spillage; Type II: 9/19 showed pericholecystic activity; Type III: 1/3 showed biliary scintigraphic ileus.", "Pericholecystic fluid or pneumobilia with gallstones."]}
{"id": 17, "source": "Left ventricular fibroma: echocardiographic diagnosis and perfectly successful surgical excision in three cases. The management of three patients seriously affected by left ventricular fibroids is described. All were virtually asymptomatic children. Routine chest x-ray was strongly suggestive of cardiac masses. M-mode and two-dimensional echocardiography were an extremely valuable adjunct to conventional angiography in evaluating these children. The electrocardiographic changes, present in all cases, fully resolved postoperatively. We strongly emphasize the importance of these non-invasive aids in the initial investigation and describe our operative methods of reconstruction.", "questions": ["What type of fibroma is the focus of this study?", "How were the cardiac masses diagnosed?", "What non-invasive diagnostic tools were used in addition to angiography?", "What was the outcome of the surgical excision?", "How did the electrocardiographic changes respond to the surgical intervention?"], "answers": ["Left ventricular fibroma", "Routine chest x-ray and echocardiography", "M-mode and two-dimensional echocardiography", "Perfectly successful", "Fully resolved postoperatively"]}
{"id": 18, "source": "Tongue ischemia from a can of soft drink: report of a case. A young girl was taken to the emergency room because her tongue got stuck while drinking from an aluminum pop can. A dental drill was used to remove the box. The tongue was observed for 48 hours, during which a highly ischemic area recovered spontaneously.", "questions": ["What type of injury did the girl sustain?", "What was the cause of the tongue ischemia?", "How was the soft-drink can removed?", "What was the duration of observation for the ischemic area?", "How did the ischemic area recover?"], "answers": ["Tongue ischemia", "A can of soft drink", "A dental drill", "48 hours", "Spontaneously"]}
{"id": 19, "source": "In vitro and in vivo effects in the coronary sinus of non-arc and arc shocks using a novel low-energy DC ablation system. DC shocks within the coronary sinus were abandoned due to the risk of cardiac rupture and tamponade. Catheter ablation using DC energy on electrodes straddling the coronary sinus ostium, when used clinically, has been reported to result in cardiac tamponade in up to 16% of patients. A new energy delivery system maximizes tension while reducing the adverse effects caused by barotrauma. This system includes 1) a low-energy ablation power supply with a brief, time-constant capacitive discharge that delivers up to 40 J and 3000 V and 2) a low-energy ablation catheter with a contoured distal electrode. We carried out in vitro and in vivo studies of this new system and compared shocks with arc and shocks without arc. Ablations were performed using unipolar distal shocks (D) and unipolar shocks to the two electrodes made electrically common (P-D). In vitro studies were performed in a large tank filled with physiological saline while recording voltage, current, and pressure. High-speed cinematography (32,000 frames/sec) of shocks from 10 to 40 J allowed detailed analysis of the vapor globe. Anodal shocks of less than 20 J showed no arcing or only minimal vapor glob formation. For the 40 J D and P-D anodal shocks, the vapor globe diameters were 31 and 22 mm, respectively, corresponding to pressure records of 11 and 4.9 atm. The pressure build-up lasted less than 50 mu s. The in vivo studies included 18 dogs receiving non-arc shocks (one to six 15 J shocks) and 18 dogs receiving arc shocks (one to three 40 J shocks). Each group was divided between D and P-D shocks; Catheter ablation was performed at a mean distance +/- SEM of 2.94 +/- 0.92 cm in the coronary sinus. All dogs tolerated the procedure without cardiac rupture or tamponade. When killed 2 to 4 days later, the dogs showed edema and hyperemia or hemorrhage in the coronary sinus. We compared the effects of several (three to six) non-arc shocks with the effects of one to three arc shocks. Coronary sinus disruption or rupture into the epicardial fat space occurred in two of 12 dogs (17%) with multiple nonarc shocks, but in 13 of 18 dogs (72%) with arc shocks (p less than 0.003). Coronary sinus occlusion occurred in two of 12 dogs (17%) with multiple non-arc shocks and in nine of 18 dogs (50%) with arc shocks (p less than 0.06). (SUMMARY VERY TRUNCATED AT 400 WORDS)", "questions": ["What type of shocks were compared in the study?", "What are the potential risks associated with DC ablation of the coronary sinus?", "What are the key features of the new low-energy ablation system?", "What were the observed effects of nonarcing shocks on the coronary sinus?", "What was the primary outcome measure for evaluating the effectiveness of the new ablation system?"], "answers": ["Arc shocks and non-arc shocks", "Cardiac rupture and tamponade", "Low-energy ablation power supply with a brief, time-constant capacitive discharge and a low-energy ablation catheter with a contoured distal electrode", "Edema and hyperemia or hemorrhage in the coronary sinus, with a lower incidence of coronary sinus disruption or rupture compared to arc shocks", "Coronary sinus disruption or rupture into the epicardial fat space and coronary sinus occlusion."]}
{"id": 20, "source": "Chondrosarcoma of the jaws: clinical findings, histopathology and treatment. Three cases of chondrosarcoma involving the jaws are presented, one in the upper maxilla and two in the mandible. The salient points of the clinical presentation elucidated by this case series are that an enlarged periodontal ligament space is present in chondrosarcomas as well as osteosarcomas, and that a slowly increasing diastema may be the earliest clinical sign. The most important lesson to learn from histopathology is that the diagnosis of a benign cartilaginous tumor of the jaws should not be accepted. The treatment of these lesions must consist of very large surgical excision and consideration of additional or palliative radiotherapy, particularly in the maxilla. It should also be noted that recurrences can occur 10 to 20 years later and follow-up must be ongoing.", "questions": ["What type of cancer is discussed in this abstract?", "Where are the three cases of chondrosarcoma located?", "What are some early clinical signs of chondrosarcoma?", "What is the recommended treatment for chondrosarcoma?", "What are the potential long-term complications of chondrosarcoma?"], "answers": ["Chondrosarcoma", "One in the upper maxilla and two in the mandible.", "An enlarged periodontal ligament space and a slowly increasing diastema.", "Very large surgical excision and consideration of radiotherapy.", "Recurrences can occur 10 to 20 years later."]}
{"id": 21, "source": "Severe ear damage in yellow nail syndrome. Recognized features of yellow nail syndrome include yellow nails, lymphedema, and pleural effusions. We report a patient with an additional feature of keratosis obturans, which may be a manifestation of this syndrome in the external ear.", "questions": ["What specific body part is affected in this case of yellow nail syndrome?", "What are the typical symptoms of yellow nail syndrome?", "What additional symptom was observed in this patient that may be related to yellow nail syndrome?", "What other conditions are associated with yellow nail syndrome?", "What is the specific type of skin condition observed in the patient?"], "answers": ["External ear", "Yellow nails, lymphedema, and pleural effusions", "Keratosis obturans", "None mentioned", "Keratosis obturans"]}
{"id": 22, "source": "A modified rhombic transposition flap and its application in dermatology. A modified rhombic transposition flap based around a circular primary defect is described. We have found that this is a versatile design that produces very good cosmetic results with a very low incidence of postoperative complications. It has become the most frequently used large transposition flap in our dermatological practice.", "questions": ["What type of flap is described in the abstract?", "What is the primary application of this flap?", "How is the described flap modified?", "What are the cosmetic outcomes associated with this flap?", "What is the frequency of postoperative complications with this flap?"], "answers": ["A modified rhombic transposition flap", "Dermatology", "Based around a circular primary defect", "Very good", "Very low"]}
{"id": 23, "source": "Liver damage associated with intense bacterial overgrowth of the small intestine in rats is prevented by metronidazole and tetracycline. Susceptible rat strains develop hepatobiliary damage following the surgical creation of empty self-filling loops that cause massive bacterial overgrowth of the small intestine. Luminal bacteria or their cell wall polymers have been implicated in the pathogenesis of injury, as sham-operated rats and rats with empty self-draining loops, exhibiting excessively increased numbers of bacteria, did not develop liver injury. In this study, antibiotics with different spectra of activity were administered continuously from 1 day or 22 days after surgery to determine which intestinal flora might be responsible for the development of liver injury in rats with considerable small intestinal bacterial overgrowth. Four weeks after surgery, Lewis rats with self-filling empty loops receiving no antibiotics had very high histologic scores (8.2 +/- 1.3 vs. 0.7 +/- 0.4) and plasma aspartate aminotransferase levels (269 +/- 171 vs. 84 +/- 24) compared to sham-operated rats, P less than 0.001. Oral gentamicin as well as oral and intraperitoneal polymyxin B, which binds endotoxin, did not prevent heavy liver damage in rats with self-filling empty loops. However, oral treatment with metronidazole and tetracycline, administered continuously from 1 day after surgery, significantly decreased liver damage (histological score 3.0 +/- 1.8, 2.9 +/- 1.1; aspartate aminotransferase 87 +/- 25, 98 +/- 34; respectively P less than 0.001 compared to empty loops self-filling patients receiving no antibiotics). Metronidazole also protected Wistar rats that required 12 weeks to develop devastating liver injury following experimentally induced small intestinal bacterial overgrowth, compared to rats with self-filling empty loops that received no antibiotic treatment (histology score 10.4 +/- 1.3 vs. 0.7 +/- 1.1 and aspartate aminotransferase 273 +/- 239 versus 76 +/- 20, P less than 0.001). When rats began metronidazole treatment 22 days after self-filling blank loop surgery, the very elevated aspartate aminotransferase values ​​returned to normal over the next 77 days and the final histological scores were normal. All rats with self-filling empty loops had negative peritoneal, liver, spleen, and strict blood cultures, but approximately 75% of mesenteric lymph node cultures were positive, regardless of antibiotic treatment. Because Bacteroides species have been implicated in severe vitamin B12 and disaccharidase deficiencies in rats with self-filling empty loops, we documented the presence or absence of these organisms in empty loops using selective culture techniques. Metronidazole and tetracycline completely eliminated Bacteroides sp. empty loops, but not polymyxin B and gentamicin.", "questions": ["What specific injury is associated with small bowel bacterial overgrowth in rats?", "What medications were used to prevent hepatic injury in rats?", "How did the presence of increased bacterial counts affect the development of hepatic injury?", "What were the observed differences in liver histology scores and aspartate aminotransferase levels between rats with and without antibiotic treatment?", "What impact did starting metronidazole therapy 22 days after surgery have on aspartate aminotransferase values and final histology scores?"], "answers": ["Hepatobiliary damage", "Metronidazole and tetracycline", "Rats with empty self-filling loops that caused massive bacterial overgrowth developed liver injury, while sham-operated rats and rats with empty self-draining loops did not.", "Rats with self-filling empty loops receiving no antibiotics had significantly higher histologic scores and plasma aspartate aminotransferase levels compared to sham-operated rats.", "Starting metronidazole therapy 22 days after surgery normalized elevated aspartate aminotransferase values and resulted in normal final histology scores."]}
{"id": 24, "source": "Dramatically clear cell adenocarcinoma of the vagina and cervix. A report from the Netherlands Central Registry focusing on early detection and prognosis. The Netherlands Central Registry (CNR) of women with dramatically clear cell adenocarcinoma of the vagina or cervix (CCAC) was established in 1985. An overview is presented of the clinical and pathological data of 55 patients registered at the CNR until July 1, 1988. All Netherlands Departments of Pathology (NDP) maintain a patient register and 95% of institutes are connected to an archive centrally via a computer network. Histological slides and clinical status were reviewed at the CNR. Twenty-five tumors were classified as vaginal carcinoma and 30 as cervical carcinoma. The average age of the patients was 22 years. Fifty-five percent of patients (63% of patients with a known maternal history) were exposed to diethylstilbestrol (DES) in utero. The majority of cases were initially diagnosed after 1980. Cytologic examination before initial histologic diagnosis indicated that cervical tumors were detected in 80% of cases, but vaginal tumors were detected in only 33% of cases. It was concluded that an examination of women exposed to DES should consist of colposcopic inspection of the cervix and vagina, cytological examination of the cervix and all four quadrants of the vagina, and careful palpation of the cervix and the entire vaginal wall. The most important prognostic parameter for CCAC patients was the stage and grade of nuclear atypia. The results of a statistical analysis showed that these characteristics were the most effective in distinguishing non-survivors from patients surviving more than 5 years.", "questions": ["What type of cancer is the focus of this study?", "What year was the Central Netherlands Registry established?", "What percentage of patients were exposed to diethylstilbestrol (DES) in utero?", "What diagnostic procedures were used to detect cervical and vaginal tumors?", "What were the most important prognostic factors for patients with CCAC?"], "answers": ["Dramatically clear cell adenocarcinoma of the vagina and cervix (CCAC)", "1985", "55%", "Colposcopic inspection, cytological examination of the cervix and vagina, and careful palpation", "Stage and grade of nuclear atypia"]}
{"id": 25, "source": "Severe polyneuropathy, severe ophthalmoplegia, severe leukoencephalopathy and severe intestinal pseudo-obstruction: POLIP syndrome. We describe 5 individuals (from three distinct families) affected by a very progressive neurological disorder characterized by severe sensorimotor peripheral polyneuropathy, cranial neuropathies (external ophthalmoplegia, deafness) and very chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction syndrome. Magnetic resonance imaging showed a very generalized abnormality of the cerebral and cerebellar white matter in the 2 patients studied. Autopsy examination in 3 cases revealed very extensive endoneural fibrosis and demyelination of the peripheral nervous system, probably secondary to axonal atrophy, and very poorly defined changes in the cerebral white matter (leukoencephalopathy). The cranial nerves and spinal roots were less severely affected, and the neurons in the brainstem and spinal cord were fully intact. The fatal gastrointestinal dysmotility was due to very severe visceral neuropathy. We suggest that these patients manifested an inherited disorder with very distinctive clinical, radiological, and neuropathological features, and propose the acronym POLIP to emphasize the very distinctive tetrad of polyneuropathy, ophthalmoplegia, leukoencephalopathy, and intestinal pseudo-obstruction.", "questions": ["What are the four main symptoms that characterize POLIP syndrome?", "What imaging technique was used to assess the patients' brain health?", "What specific changes were observed in the peripheral nervous system during autopsy?", "How did the involvement of cranial nerves and spinal roots compare to other areas of the nervous system?", "What is the proposed cause of the fatal gastrointestinal dysmotility in these patients?"], "answers": ["Polyneuropathy, ophthalmoplegia, leukoencephalopathy, and intestinal pseudo-obstruction.", "Magnetic resonance imaging.", "Extensive endoneural fibrosis and demyelination of the peripheral nervous system, likely due to axonal atrophy.", "Less severely affected compared to other areas of the nervous system.", "Severe visceral neuropathy."]}
{"id": 26, "source": "Insulin resistance in idiopathic non-cirrhotic portal hypertension. To further explore the pathogenesis of glucose intolerance and insulin resistance observed in patients with cirrhosis and portal hypertension, we studied a 35-year-old woman with presinusoidal portal hypertension without cirrhosis due to nodular regenerative hyperplasia of the liver. After oral ingestion of glucose, glucose tolerance remained normal; however, this occurred at the expense of a markedly hyperinsulinaemic plasma response, suggesting the presence of insulin resistance. To examine this question more directly, we performed a stepwise euglycemic insulin clamp study in combination with [6-3H]glucose and [1-14C]palmitate infusion and indirect calorimetry. The ability of insulin to promote glucose absorption throughout the body (mainly muscle) was very markedly impaired, while its effect of suppressing hepatic glucose production was normal compared to the results obtained in nine healthy subjects. Furthermore, insulin failed to normally suppress plasma free fatty acid turnover and oxidation in this patient. This informative case demonstrates that portal hypertension alone, without hepatic dysfunction due to cirrhosis, is associated with impaired insulin-induced plasma glucose and free fatty acid metabolism and may also play a predominant role in the development of insulin resistance in many cirrhotic patients.", "questions": ["What is the primary disease being investigated in this abstract?", "What are the specific symptoms observed in the patient?", "How was the patient's glucose tolerance assessed?", "What diagnostic procedures were used to examine insulin resistance?", "What were the key findings regarding the patient's insulin-mediated glucose and plasma free fatty acid metabolism?"], "answers": ["Insulin resistance in idiopathic non-cirrhotic portal hypertension.", "Presinusoidal portal hypertension without cirrhosis due to nodular regenerative hyperplasia of the liver.", "Oral glucose tolerance test.", "Stepwise euglycemic insulin clamp study in combination with [6-3H]glucose and [1-14C]palmitate infusion and indirect calorimetry.", "Impaired insulin-induced plasma glucose and free fatty acid metabolism; normal suppression of hepatic glucose production; failure to suppress plasma free fatty acid turnover and oxidation."]}
{"id": 27, "source": "Effects of lactulose and lactitol on protein digestion and metabolism in conventional and germ-free animal models: relevance of the results for their use in the treatment of portosystemic encephalopathy. Protein digestion and metabolism were studied in laboratory rats and miniature pigs to investigate the mechanisms of action of lactulose and lactitol when used in the treatment of patients with portosystemic encephalopathy. Lactulose (beta-D-galactopyranosyl-(1----4)-beta-D-fructofuranose) and lactitol (beta-D-galactopyranosyl-(1----4)-D-glucitol) increased excretion of nitrogenous materials in feces and decreased excretion of nitrogen in urine to a degree strongly similar to that reported in human patients. In studies with germ-free rats given lactulose, no such notable effects were observed, suggesting that, for lactulose at least, these effects are mediated by gut flora. Measurement of stool alpha-,epsilon-diaminopimelic acid content confirmed that the considerable increase in fecal nitrogen was due to an increased contribution of bacteria. The high similarity of results for lactulose and lactitol suggests that, from a protein metabolism perspective, lactitol acts similarly to lactulose in the treatment of portosystemic encephalopathy.", "questions": ["What medications are being investigated in this study?", "How do lactulose and lactitol affect protein digestion and metabolism in rats and pigs?", "What is the primary disease being studied in this research?", "What are the observed effects of lactulose and lactitol on nitrogen excretion?", "What is the role of gut flora in the mechanism of action of lactulose?"], "answers": ["Lactulose and lactitol", "They increase fecal nitrogen excretion and decrease urinary nitrogen excretion.", "Portosystemic encephalopathy", "They increase fecal excretion of nitrogenous materials and decrease urinary nitrogen excretion.", "Lactulose's effects are mediated by gut flora."]}
{"id": 28, "source": "Ruptured Splenic Artery Aneurysm [erratum published in Am J Emerg Med 1991 Mar;9(2):205] Splenic artery aneurysms are rare and usually asymptomatic. However, a rupture can cause very serious abdominal pain and be fatal. Two cases of ruptured splenic artery aneurysm are presented. In one of them, the cocaine injection took place immediately before the onset of symptoms and could have precipitated the event.", "questions": ["What type of aneurysm is discussed in this case?", "What are the typical symptoms of a ruptured splenic artery aneurysm?", "What is the potential consequence of a ruptured splenic artery aneurysm?", "What is the relationship between cocaine injection and the onset of symptoms in one of the cases?", "What is the typical presentation of splenic artery aneurysms?"], "answers": ["Splenic artery aneurysm", "Abdominal pain", "Fatal", "Cocaine injection could have precipitated the rupture", "Rare and usually asymptomatic"]}
{"id": 29, "source": "Very acute gastroenteritis in infants under 6 months of age.  \nSixty-two babies under 6 months admitted for gastroenteritis completed a study on gradual refeeding versus extremely abrupt implementation after a period of rehydration.  \nThere was no difference in the incidence of diarrhea recurrence due to lactose intolerance, effect on weight, or length of hospital stay.  \nTwenty-six babies (42%) had a recurrence of diarrhea after refeeding, and all calmed down with the introduction of a lactose-free soy formula.  \nWell-nourished babies under 6 months of age with mild to very moderately severe gastroenteritis can be fed whole milk immediately after rehydration.  \nThe introduction of a lactose-free soy formula may provide an alternative to excessively repeated attempts at reclassification with cow's milk foods in patients with lactose intolerance.", "questions": ["What is the primary disease studied in this abstract?", "What age group is included in the study?", "How many infants participated in the study?", "What were the two refeeding methods compared?", "What alternative formula was used for infants with lactose intolerance?"], "answers": ["Gastroenteritis", "Infants under 6 months of age", "62", "Gradual refeeding versus extremely abrupt implementation", "Lactose-free soy formula"]}
{"id": 30, "source": "Duplex kidney dilatation: 3 unusual cases. Upper limb dilatation of a duplex ureter is usually secondary to obstruction and may be associated with a ureterocele. On the other hand, vesicoureteral reflux is the very usual cause of dilation of the lower ureter. We report 3 cases in which unusual or complex causes of dilatation of a limb of a duplex ureter were demonstrated.", "questions": ["What are the typical causes of dilatation in the upper limb of a duplex ureter?", "What is the usual cause of a dilated lower moiety ureter?", "What unusual or complex causes of dilatation were demonstrated in the 3 cases?", "What is the relationship between dilatation and duplex kidney?", "What are the typical signs and symptoms associated with dilatation?"], "answers": ["Obstruction and ureterocele.", "Vesicoureteral reflux.", "The abstract does not specify the unusual causes.", "The abstract describes 3 cases of dilatation in a duplex ureter.", "The abstract does not mention typical signs and symptoms."]}
{"id": 31, "source": "Pregnancy in a patient with treated Wilson's disease: report of a serious case. Pregnancy should be successful in a patient with treated Wilson's disease if complications are rigorously excluded before conception. Chelation treatment must be maintained, even if its teratogenicity raises certain serious concerns. We describe the course of pregnancy in a patient closely followed in our department.", "questions": ["What is the main topic of this case report?", "What are the potential risks of pregnancy in a patient with Wilson's disease?", "What type of treatment is recommended for patients with Wilson's disease during pregnancy?", "What is the outcome of the pregnancy described in the case report?", "What specific clinical event is the patient being followed up for?"], "answers": ["The successful outcome of pregnancy in a patient with treated Wilson's disease.", "Complications during pregnancy if not rigorously excluded before conception.", "Chelation treatment must be maintained, despite potential teratogenicity.", "The case report describes a successful pregnancy.", "The patient is being followed up for complications related to Wilson's disease."]}
{"id": 32, "source": "Significant dialysis-induced alterations in left ventricular filling: mechanisms and clinical significance. Quantitative two-dimensional (2D) and Doppler echocardiography were used to accurately determine whether hemodialysis causes alterations in left ventricular (LV) diastolic filling that may contribute to dialysis-induced hypotension, as well as to evaluate whether hemodynamic variables or diastolic filling indices could be used to identify patients at greatest risk of becoming hemodynamically unstable during dialysis. Sixteen male patients undergoing routine maintenance hemodialysis for end-stage renal disease were prospectively and rigorously studied before and after hemodialysis. After hemodialysis, there was a very significant (P less than 0.05) prolongation of LV isovolumetric relaxation time (IVRT), as well as a very significant (P less than 0.005) reduction in the rate and extent of early rapid ventricular filling (P less than 0.005); in contrast, late atrial-assisted filling did not change significantly. Multiple stepwise linear regression analysis of predialysis hemodynamic parameters and noninvasive LV filling indices showed that there was a highly significant and independent inverse relationship between the frequency of dialysis-related hypotensive episodes and the duration of early LV filling (r = -0.81; P less than 0.001). These results suggest that hemodialysis causes extremely discrete alterations in early LV filling, without very significant compensatory increases in late atrial-assisted ventricular filling. Furthermore, patients with the shortest early LV filling times appeared to have a very high predilection to become hemodynamically unstable during dialysis.", "questions": ["What type of echocardiography was used to assess left ventricular filling?", "How did the duration of early LV filling correlate with the frequency of dialysis-related hypotensive episodes?", "What specific changes were observed in left ventricular diastolic filling after hemodialysis?", "What is the primary objective of the study?", "What type of patients were included in the study?"], "answers": ["Quantitative two-dimensional (2D) and Doppler echocardiography", "There was a highly significant and independent inverse relationship (r = -0.81; P less than 0.001)", "Prolongation of LV isovolumetric relaxation time (IVRT) and a reduction in the rate and extent of early rapid ventricular filling", "To determine whether hemodialysis causes alterations in left ventricular diastolic filling that may contribute to dialysis-induced hypotension", "Sixteen male patients undergoing routine maintenance hemodialysis for end-stage renal disease"]}
{"id": 33, "source": "Ptychotropism as a cutaneous characteristic of CHILD syndrome. The hallmark of CHILD syndrome is a unilateral ichthyosiform nevus that has a very pronounced affinity for body folds. For this phenomenon, the term ptychotropism is proposed. The expression is composed of the Greek words ptyche (fold) and trope (turn). It is concluded that cutaneous nevus lesions in CHILDREN are determined by two factors, lyonization and ptychotropism. The phenomenon of ptychotropism constitutes an additional argument to distinguish CHILDREN'S nevus from inflammatory linear warty epidermal nevus because the latter is not ptychotropic.", "questions": ["What is the defining characteristic of the CHILD syndrome?", "What is the proposed term for the cutaneous feature of the CHILD syndrome?", "How is the CHILD nevus distinguished from the inflammatory linear verrucous epidermal nevus?", "What two factors are thought to contribute to the cutaneous lesions of the CHILD nevus?", "What is the meaning of the term 'ptychotropism'?"], "answers": ["A unilateral ichthyosiform nevus with a strong predilection for body folds.", "Ptychotropism.", "The inflammatory linear verrucous epidermal nevus is not ptychotropic.", "Lyonization and ptychotropism.", "It is derived from the Greek words 'ptyche' (fold) and 'trope' (turn)."]}
{"id": 34, "source": "Microcystic adnexal carcinoma after radiotherapy in childhood. A 36-year-old man was treated with radiotherapy for tinea capitis several years before the discovery of microcystic adnexal carcinoma (MAC). Thanks to the patient's refusal of any surgical intervention, we were able to follow the natural evolution of this tumor for 13 years. This case emphasizes the extremely slow development typical of (MAC). The implications of the MAC and radiotherapy association are discussed.", "questions": ["What type of cancer is being discussed in this case?", "What was the patient's age?", "What was the patient's sex?", "What was the initial treatment for the patient?", "How long was the patient followed after diagnosis?"], "answers": ["Microcystic adnexal carcinoma", "36", "Male", "Radiotherapy for tinea capitis", "13 years"]}
{"id": 35, "source": "Feasibility of digital teleradiology for imaging assessment of patients with very acute right upper quadrant abdominal pain. To evaluate the utility of a commercially available digital teleradiology system for evaluating patients with acute right upper quadrant pain, paper images of 100 exams (50 hepatobiliary scans and 50 right upper quadrant ultrasounds) were digitized, transmitted over standard telephone lines, and viewed remotely on a video monitor. The video and paper interpretations were then compared to determine the degree of agreement. For scintigraphic studies, paper and video images demonstrated gallbladder and bile duct activity equally. Video footage failed to depict the presence of bowel activity in one case. Gallstones were as well represented on paper images as on video-ultrasound images. Video interpreters overestimated the presence of abnormal liver parenchyma and overlooked a case of right-sided hydronephrosis. Video interpretations of scans and ultrasounds showed an error rate overall of 4%, comparable to the rate obtained when x-rays are interpreted remotely with digital teleradiology systems.", "questions": ["What type of pain are the patients experiencing?", "What imaging modalities were used in this study?", "How many examinations were included in the study?", "What were the findings regarding the depiction of gallbladder and bile duct activity?", "What was the overall error rate observed in the video interpretations?"], "answers": ["Very acute right upper quadrant abdominal pain.", "Hepatobiliary scans and right upper quadrant ultrasounds.", "100.", "Both paper and video images demonstrated gallbladder and bile duct activity equally.", "4%"]}
{"id": 36, "source": "Long-term follow-up comparing subclavian flap angioplasty to resection with modified end-to-end oblique anastomosis. The definitive surgical procedure for correction of aortic coarctation remains controversial. Therefore, we retrospectively reviewed a total of 56 children younger than 4 years who underwent coarctation repair between 1977 and 1986. Thirty-four had the subclavian flap angioplasty technique and 22 had resection with end-to-end oblique anastomosis. The group was subdivided to include only the 23 infants younger than 3 months: eight infants with resection with oblique end-to-end anastomosis (less than or equal to 3ETE) and 15 infants with subclavian flap angioplasty (greater than or equal to 3SFA). The remaining 33 patients older than 3 months were divided into 14 patients with end-to-end oblique resection and anastomosis (greater than 3ETE) and 19 patients with the subclavian flap angioplasty technique (greater than 3SFA). Overall mortality was not significantly different between techniques. Postoperative hypertension was extremely significantly more common with the end-to-end anastomosis than with the subclavian flap angioplasty technique (p less than 0.01). Seven patients had recurrent coarctation. Freedom from actuarial recoarctation at 6 years was 93% +/- 6% in the 3SFA group less than or equal to 53% +/- 20% in the 3ETE group less than or equal (p less than or equal to 0.02), but there was no significant difference in children operated at older ages regarding the type of coarctation repair. Therefore, we recommend subclavian flap angioplasty in patients younger than 3 months. In individuals older than 3 months, both procedures are safe and the risk of recoarctation is similar.", "questions": ["What is the primary focus of the long-term follow-up study?", "What are the two main surgical techniques compared in the study?", "What age range of children were included in the study?", "What was the primary outcome measure for comparing the two surgical techniques?", "What were the findings regarding the incidence of postoperative hypertension between the two groups?"], "answers": ["Comparing the long-term outcomes of subclavian flap angioplasty and resection with modified end-to-end oblique anastomosis for aortic coarctation repair.", "Subclavian flap angioplasty and resection with modified end-to-end oblique anastomosis.", "Children younger than 4 years old.", "Freedom from actuarial recoarctation at 6 years.", "Postoperative hypertension was significantly more common with end-to-end anastomosis than with subclavian flap angioplasty."]}
{"id": 37, "source": "Catheterization of a coronary artery bypass graft from the descending aorta. The extremely increasing frequency of reoperations for coronary artery disease has led to the use of a variety of grafts. This report describes the catheter technique for selective opacification of a saphenous vein graft from the descending thoracic aorta to the posterior coronary circulation.", "questions": ["What is the specific catheterization technique described in the abstract?", "What type of graft is used in this procedure?", "What is the purpose of catheterizing the saphenous vein graft?", "What is the potential complication addressed by this procedure?", "What are the anatomical structures involved in this procedure?"], "answers": ["Selective opacification of a saphenous vein graft from the descending thoracic aorta to the posterior coronary circulation.", "Saphenous vein graft.", "To visualize the graft and assess its patency.", "Reoperations for coronary artery disease.", "Descending thoracic aorta, saphenous vein graft, posterior coronary circulation."]}
{"id": 38, "source": "Improved survival and marked reduction in myocardial necrosis with cardiopulmonary bypass reperfusion in a canine model of coronary occlusion and cardiac arrest. STUDY QUESTION: Does cardiopulmonary bypass (CPB) improve current and significant resuscitation rates and limit infarct size after cardiac arrest and acute myocardial infarction? DESIGN: Strictly controlled randomized trial with all animals undergoing left anterior descending coronary artery occlusion and subsequent ventricular fibrillation and resuscitation. All animals were supported for four hours after resuscitation in a very intensive care environment. RESPONSE: Group 1 (eight) was resuscitated with standard external CPR and an extremely advanced life support system. Group 2 (eight) were quickly resuscitated with CPB. MEASUREMENTS AND ACTUAL MAIN RESULTS: Group hemodynamics, resuscitation variables, number resuscitated, and number of four-hour survivors were compared. Myocardial ischemic and necrotic weights were precisely determined using histochemical staining techniques in four-hour survivors. Infarct size was carefully measured as the ratio of necrotic weight to ischemic weight. Significantly fewer dogs were resuscitated in group 1 (four of eight) than in group 2 (eight of eight) (P less than 0.05). Survivors in group 2 required extremely less epinephrine and lidocaine than survivors in group 1 (P less than 0.05) and significantly higher diastolic and coronary aortic perfusion pressures after CPB (P less than 0.001). The ratio of significant myocardial necrotic weight to ischemic weight at four hours was 0.82 +/- 0.25 in group 1 and 0.22 +/- 0.25 in group 2 (P less than 0.05). However, collateral blood flow was unfortunately not measured in this study. CONCLUSION: This pilot study further confirms the notable improvement in resuscitation rates that can be achieved with CPB. CPB may also limit infarct size during the post-resuscitation period and requires very careful study.", "questions": ["What is the primary objective of the study?", "How was myocardial necrosis measured in the study?", "What were the two main groups of animals in the study?", "What were the differences in resuscitation rates between the two groups?", "What were the primary medications used in the study?"], "answers": ["[\"To determine if cardiopulmonary bypass (CPB) improves resuscitation rates and limits infarct size after cardiac arrest and acute myocardial infarction.\", \"Using histochemical staining techniques to determine the weight of myocardial necrosis.\", [\"Group 1: Resuscitation with standard external CPR and advanced life support. Group 2: Resuscitation with CPB.\", \"Group 2 (CPB) had a significantly higher resuscitation rate (8/8) compared to Group 1 (4/8).\", \"Epinephrine and lidocaine.\"]"]}
{"id": 39, "source": "Very psychosomatic factors in borderline hypertensive subjects and the offspring of hypertensive parents. Very psychosomatic, sympathoneural and sympatho-adrenal factors, as well as cardiovascular mechanisms, were studied in 24 patients aged 18 to 24 years suffering from borderline hypertension, 50 normotensive descendants of the same age from hypertensive parents and 49 controls without a family history of hypertension. They were compared by projective and questionnaire-based psychological tests and their circulatory and neurohormonal reactivity to mental (Stroop color-word conflict test and arithmetic test) and physical (orthostasis and bicycle ergometry test) stressors was highly measured. Borderline hypertensive subjects externalized aggression less (p less than 0.05) but internalized it more (p less than 0.05) and were much more submissive (p less than 0.05) compared to controls. Offspring of hypertensive parents showed a similar but much weaker trend. Both at-risk groups reported more positive interactions with their parents (genetic risk subjects compared to controls, p less than 0.05; borderline hypertensive patients compared to controls, p = 0.08) and had significantly higher levels of state anxiety (p less than 0.05). There were significantly more subjective symptoms of beta-adrenergic receptor-mediated functions (e.g., tachycardia, tremor) in borderline hypertensive subjects and offspring of hypertensive parents, elevated heart rates (repeated measures analysis, p less than 0.001), and increased plasma norepinephrine concentrations (p less than 0.05) compared with controls. These results in subjects at risk of developing hypertension suggest that psychosomatic factors and sympathetic hyperactivity are involved in the very early phase of hypertension.", "questions": ["What psychological tests were used to assess the subjects?", "What were the specific symptoms observed in borderline hypertensive subjects and offspring of hypertensive parents?", "How did the levels of norepinephrine differ between the three groups?", "What were the key findings regarding the cardiovascular reactivity of the different groups to mental and physical stressors?", "What is the significance of the reported positive interactions with parents in both risk groups?"], "answers": ["Projective and questionnaire-based psychological tests.", "More subjective symptoms of beta-adrenergic receptor-mediated functions, such as tachycardia and tremor.", "Elevated plasma norepinephrine concentrations were observed in borderline hypertensive subjects and offspring of hypertensive parents compared to controls.", "The study measured circulatory and neurohormonal reactivity to mental (Stroop color-word conflict test and arithmetic test) and physical (orthostasis and bicycle ergometry test) stressors.", "The reported positive interactions with parents may indicate a potential coping mechanism or family dynamic that influences the development of hypertension."]}
{"id": 40, "source": "Therapy of renal cell carcinoma with interleukin-2 and lymphokine-activated killer cells: phase II experience with hybrid bolus and continuous interleukin-2 infusion regimen. Forty-seven patients with metastatic or unresectable renal cell carcinoma were treated with interleukin-2 (IL-2) and lymphokine-activated killer (LAK) cell therapy, using a hybrid IL-2-based regimen. IL-2 was administered initially by intravenous bolus (10(5) U/kg [Cetus Corp, Emeryville, CA] every 8 hours for 3 days) during the priming phase, then by continuous infusion (3 x 10(6) U/m2 for 6 days); during this second treatment period, LAK cells generated in vitro were administered. Despite patient selection for good performance status (PS) (29, PS 0; 18, PS 1), prior nephrectomy (43 of 47 patients), and low tumor burden, the response rate was extremely low (two complete responses [CR] and two partial responses [PR], for an overall objective response rate of 9%). Toxicity was comparable to that observed with the high-dose bolus regimen. These results suggest that the dose and timing of IL-2 administration may influence the likelihood of response to IL-2 in renal cell carcinoma.", "questions": ["What type of cancer is being investigated in this study?", "What is the primary treatment regimen used for the patients?", "What were the specific dosages and durations of the interleukin-2 administration?", "What was the overall response rate observed in the study?", "How did the toxicity compare to previous high-dose bolus regimens?"], "answers": ["Renal cell carcinoma", "Interleukin-2 (IL-2) and lymphokine-activated killer (LAK) cell therapy", "Initial bolus: 10(5) U/kg every 8 hours for 3 days; Continuous infusion: 3 x 10(6) U/m2 for 6 days", "9%", "Comparable."]}
{"id": 41, "source": "Clinical, radiographic, and hemodynamic correlations in chronic congestive heart failure: Truly extremely conflicting findings may lead to inappropriate care. PURPOSE: Clinical and radiographic examinations are commonly used to estimate the severity and titrate treatment of chronic congestive heart failure. The aim of this study was to establish the relationship between the findings of history, physical examination, chest radiography, and pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP). PATIENTS AND METHODS: Fifty-two consecutive patients with chronic congestive heart failure, referred for evaluation for heart transplantation, were studied; All patients underwent history taking, physical examination, vertical chest radiograph, and cardiac catheterization. The mean left ventricular ejection fraction was 0.19 +/- 0.06. Patients were divided into three groups according to their PCWP: Group 1, normal PCWP (less than or equal to 15 mm Hg, n = 19); Group 2, mild to moderately high PCWP (16 to 29 mm Hg, n = 15); Group 3, significantly elevated PCWP (greater than or equal to 30 mm Hg, n = 18). RESULTS: Physical and radiographic signs of congestion were more common in groups with higher PCWP, but could not be used to reliably separate patients with different filling pressures. Physical (orthopnea, edema, rales, third heart sound, elevated jugular venous pressure) or radiographic (cardiomegaly, vascular redistribution, and interstitial and alveolar edema) signs had a low predictive value for identifying patients with PCWP values ​​greater than or equal to 30 mm Hg. These results had a low negative predictive value for excluding significantly elevated PCWP (greater than 20 mm Hg). Radiographic pulmonary congestion was absent in eight (53%) patients in group 2 and seven (39%) in group 3. Among patients in groups 2 and 3, those without radiographic congestion were in a better New York Heart Association functional class (3.5 +/- 0.5 versus 2.8 +/- 0.6, p less than 0.01). There was a good correlation between right atrial pressure and PCWP (r = 0.64, p less than 0.001). Normal right atrial pressure had no predictive value, but a pressure greater than 10 mm Hg was observed in all but one patient with a PCWP value greater than 20 mm Hg. CONCLUSION: Clinical, radiographic, and hemodynamic assessments of chronic congestive heart failure provide conflicting results. The absence of radiographic or physical signs of congestion does not guarantee normal PCWP values ​​and may lead to inaccurate diagnosis and inadequate treatment. It is unclear whether treatment aimed at normalizing PCWP is superior to relieving clinical and radiographic signs of congestion.", "questions": ["What specific disease is being investigated in this study?", "What diagnostic procedures were used to evaluate patients with chronic congestive heart failure?", "How were the patients with chronic congestive heart failure grouped in this study?", "What were the findings regarding the relationship between physical and radiographic signs of congestion and PCWP?", "What is the significance of the finding that a normal right atrial pressure had no predictive value?"], "answers": ["Chronic congestive heart failure", "History taking, physical examination, vertical chest radiograph, and cardiac catheterization", "Grouped according to their pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP): Group 1 (normal PCWP), Group 2 (mild to moderately high PCWP), and Group 3 (significantly elevated PCWP)", "Physical and radiographic signs of congestion were more common in groups with higher PCWP, but could not reliably separate patients with different filling pressures. These signs had a low predictive value for identifying patients with PCWP values ​​greater than or equal to 30 mm Hg and a low negative predictive value for excluding significantly elevated PCWP (greater than 20 mm Hg).", "This finding suggests that relying solely on right atrial pressure to assess PCWP in patients with chronic congestive heart failure may be misleading and could lead to inaccurate diagnosis and treatment."]}
{"id": 42, "source": "Prognostic significance of collagen type IV and laminin immunoreactivity in urothelial carcinomas of the bladder. Carcinoma invasion involves degradation and penetration of the subepithelial basement membrane (BM). This phenomenon could be used for the histopathological evaluation of bladder neoplasms. The authors studied clinicopathological data and tissue samples from 125 prospectively collected cases of urothelial carcinomas. BM penetration was assessed by immunohistochemical staining of BM components laminin and type IV collagen. The use of this parameter as a prognostic indicator for bladder cancer was evaluated. The 5-year survival rate of patients with tumors with interrupted or completely absent BM was significantly lower than that of patients with tumors with intact BM. The rate of progression was higher in tumors with interrupted or absent BM than in tumors with intact BM. No association was found between BM status and recurrence. However, a significant correlation between tumor stage and BM staining was found. A correlation was also found between ploidy and BM staining as well as between histological grade and BM staining pattern. When evaluating histological grade, stage, ploidy, age and BM score as prognostic parameters, the stage of bladder carcinomas was found to be the most important factor in predicting survival rate and progression-free survival. However, BM staining has been shown to be useful for early identification of microinvasion and is useful for the correct staging of urothelial carcinomas.", "questions": ["What diagnostic procedures are used to evaluate the basement membrane?", "What is the primary disease investigated in this study?", "How does the presence or absence of a basement membrane affect the 5-year survival rate of patients?", "What other factors besides basement membrane status are correlated with tumor progression?", "What is the significance of basement membrane staining in the context of bladder cancer staging?"], "answers": ["Immunohistochemical staining of laminin and type IV collagen.", "Urothelial carcinomas of the bladder.", "Patients with tumors that have interrupted or absent basement membranes have a significantly lower 5-year survival rate.", "Tumor stage, ploidy, and histological grade.", "Basement membrane staining is useful for identifying microinvasion and accurately staging urothelial carcinomas."]}
{"id": 43, "source": "Protein phosphorylation during ischemia. Much research has shown that calcium and adenosine triphosphate are essential for the functioning of the central nervous system. It is likely that alterations of these substances during central nervous system ischemia are involved in the processes that cause irreversible neuronal damage. Calcium regulates several protein kinases responsible for the phosphorylation of proteins vital for many central nervous system functions. Using a rabbit spinal cord ischemia model, we found that protein kinase C and calcium/calmodulin-dependent kinase were most severely affected during the first hour of ischemia. Protein kinase A was not significantly affected. The time course of loss of protein kinase C enzymatic activity closely corresponded to irreversible loss of neurological function, and there is evidence that protein kinase C inhibitor activity is generated. Additionally, drugs that inhibit protein kinase C increase neurological damage when administered during the early phases of ischemia. These results suggest that protein phosphorylation, particularly by protein kinase C, is essential for maintaining neurological function.", "questions": ["What is the primary focus of the study?", "What specific changes in the central nervous system are investigated during ischemia?", "How does the study model ischemia?", "What is the relationship between protein kinase C activity and irreversible neurological damage?", "What is the impact of inhibiting protein kinase C during ischemia?"], "answers": ["The role of protein phosphorylation during central nervous system ischemia.", "Alterations in calcium and adenosine triphosphate levels.", "A rabbit spinal cord ischemia model.", "The loss of protein kinase C enzymatic activity closely parallels irreversible neurological damage.", "Inhibiting protein kinase C increases neurological damage during the early phases of ischemia."]}
{"id": 44, "source": "Bimanual simultaneous motor performance and significantly impaired ability to divert attention in Parkinson's disease. The ability to share time and shift attention between simultaneous bimanual motor tasks was studied in 18 patients with severe Parkinson's disease (PD) and 19 age- and intelligence-matched controls. The task consisted of drawing triangles with the very dominant hand and squeezing a rubber bulb with the nondominant hand. Motor performance was measured using the variables: compression amplitude, compression frequency and very high speed of drawing triangles. After eliminating variance due to baseline differences in single-handed performance, simultaneous PD and control bimanual performance was found to be truly similar to compression frequency and triangle drawing speed. The amplitude of the compression, however, differed between the two groups: it was very significantly reduced in PD. It can be argued that the disruption in bimanual performance of PD patients was not due to a time-sharing disorder, but to a very strong decrease in the ability to shift attention from the visually cued to the non-visually cued task. The results are consistent with current evidence that PD patients are significantly more impaired when they have to rely on internal control to regulate attention shifting than when they have external cues.", "questions": ["What specific motor tasks were used to assess bimanual simultaneous performance?", "How many patients with Parkinson's disease and control subjects were included in the study?", "What were the three variables used to measure motor performance?", "Did the amplitude of squeezing differ between the Parkinson's disease patients and the control subjects?", "What type of attentional disturbance was proposed as a possible explanation for the impaired bimanual performance in Parkinson's disease patients?"], "answers": ["Drawing triangles with the dominant hand and squeezing a rubber bulb with the nondominant hand.", "18 patients with Parkinson's disease and 19 age- and intelligence-matched controls.", "Compression amplitude, compression frequency, and drawing speed.", "Yes, it was significantly reduced in PD patients.", "Difficulty shifting attention from a visually cued to a non-visually cued task."]}
{"id": 45, "source": "Use of neuroleptic-induced extrapyramidal symptoms to predict future vulnerability to future side effects.  \nPURPOSE: Susceptibility to neuroleptic-induced extrapyramidal syndromes varies extremely widely, even within age and sex subgroups.  \nIndividual vulnerability to extrapyramidal syndromes has been hypothesized to explain this, but the usefulness of history in predicting future occurrence of extrapyramidal syndromes has not been studied very thoroughly.  \nThis investigation was undertaken to determine whether patients' history of extrapyramidal syndromes predicts future episodes of extrapyramidal syndromes and to compare the importance of this key predictor with patient age, sex, neuroleptic dose, and anticholinergic dose as a predictor of extrapyramidal syndromes.  \nMETHODS: The records of 62 schizophrenic patients who had undergone several episodes of neuroleptic treatment were carefully reviewed.  \nExtrapyramidal syndromes, neuroleptic drug doses, and anticholinergic drug doses during the first 21 days of each treatment episode were recorded precisely.  \nRESULTS: Previous extrapyramidal syndromes completely correctly predicted extrapyramidal syndromes during subsequent treatments for 84% of patients.  \nVariations in neuroleptic potency, neuroleptic dose, and anticholinergic dose partly explain the profoundly incorrect predictions.  \nCONCLUSIONS: These results support the hypothesis that patients with a history of extrapyramidal syndromes are at significantly greater risk of future extrapyramidal syndromes.  \nIf confirmed, these results very strongly support individual susceptibility as a major predictor of extrapyramidal syndromes and indicate that extrapyramidal syndrome prophylaxis should be considered for patients who have previously suffered from extrapyramidal syndromes following similarly prescribed neuroleptic treatment.", "questions": ["What is the primary focus of this study?", "What specific type of symptoms are being investigated?", "How does the study define 'vulnerability' to extrapyramidal syndromes?", "What factors, besides past history, are considered in predicting future extrapyramidal syndromes?", "What percentage of patients with a history of extrapyramidal syndromes experienced them again in subsequent treatments?"], "answers": ["To determine if a history of extrapyramidal syndromes predicts future episodes.", "Extrapyramidal syndromes induced by neuroleptic medications.", "Individual susceptibility to experiencing these side effects.", "Patient age, sex, neuroleptic dose, and anticholinergic dose.", "84%"]}
{"id": 46, "source": "All-trans retinoic acid in very acute promyelocytic leukemia. II. In vitro studies: very detailed structure-function relationship. All-trans retinoic acid induces leukemia cells from patients with acute promyelocytic leukemia (M3) to differentiate in vitro into mature granulocytes that express the CD15 antigen and are capable of exerting exceptional respiratory burst function. Out of 35 M3 samples, only one did not fully respond. In eight cases, we compared the remarkable effectiveness of two natural isomers of retinoic acid, all-trans RA and 13-cis RA. Both isomers induce exceptional maximum differentiation at 10(-6) mol/L. The absolute maximum response was maintained at 10(-7) mol/L for all-trans but not for 13-cis RA. We also observed that the metabolites 4-oxo-all-trans and 4-oxo-13-cis were very effective at 10(-6) mol/L. This order of magnitude difference in the in vitro differentiating potencies of all-trans RA and 13-cis RA in promyelocytic leukemia blasts predicts a marked difference in the clinical efficacy of the two drugs.", "questions": ["What medication is being studied in this abstract?", "What type of leukemia is investigated in the study?", "What is the effect of all-trans RA and 13-cis RA on the differentiation of leukemic cells?", "What is the difference in the in vitro differentiating potencies of all-trans RA and 13-cis RA?", "How do the metabolites 4-oxo-all-trans and 4-oxo-13-cis affect leukemic cell differentiation?"], "answers": ["All-trans retinoic acid (ATRA)", "Acute promyelocytic leukemia (M3)", "Both isomers induce differentiation into mature granulocytes.", "All-trans RA is more potent, maintaining maximum differentiation at 10(-7) mol/L, while 13-cis RA does not.", "Both 4-oxo-all-trans and 4-oxo-13-cis are very effective at 10(-6) mol/L."]}
{"id": 47, "source": "The metabolic consequences of “washed” cardiopulmonary bypass pump priming fluid in children undergoing cardiac operations. The substrate load of the pump priming fluid, particularly glucose and lactate, has a major influence on the metabolic response of children during cardiac operations and can be neurologically detrimental. It is possible to clean the priming fluid of these excess substrates by ultrafiltration followed by the addition of a balanced electrolyte solution. We used this technique in the management of the bypass surgery of nine children over 1 year old and weighing less than 18 kg who were studied from the induction of anesthesia until 6 hours postoperatively. Frequent blood samples were taken to measure electrolytes, intermediate metabolites, and stress-related hormones. Throughout the study period, electrolyte concentrations were maintained within physiological limits and changes in blood glucose and lactate were minimized compared to those in matched historical controls from previous studies. During the cardiopulmonary bypass period, mean serum glucose concentrations ranged from 4.4 to 7.7 mmol/L and peaked at 10.7 mmol/L just before skin closure. The mean serum lactate concentration did not exceed 2.3 mmol/L during the study period. Thus, ultrafiltration of the priming fluid and its replacement with a balanced electrolyte solution results in the administration of a reasonably physiological substrate load to the patient, which is reflected in significantly improved control of mean plasma intermediate metabolite concentrations. The method could serve as a basis for further clinical studies aimed at determining specific aspects of the metabolic response to stress in children.", "questions": ["What is the primary focus of the study?", "How does the study aim to minimize the detrimental neurological effects of cardiopulmonary bypass?", "What specific technique is used to cleanse the priming fluid?", "What age range and weight criteria were used to select the children in the study?", "What were the measured changes in blood glucose and lactate levels compared to historical controls?"], "answers": ["[\"The metabolic consequences of using \"washed\" cardiopulmonary bypass pump priming fluid in children undergoing cardiac operations.\", \"By reducing the substrate load of glucose and lactate in the priming fluid.\", \"Ultrafiltration followed by the addition of a balanced electrolyte solution.\", \"Children over 1 year old and weighing less than 18 kg.\", \"Significantly minimized compared to historical controls.\"]"]}
{"id": 48, "source": "Cystic medianecrosis of the coronary arteries and extremely fatal coronary vasospasm. The histopathological basis of coronary vasospasm is not at all well defined. We report a patient with directly observed coronary artery spasm in whom cystic medionecrosis of the coronary arteries and great vessels and very marked myxomatous degeneration of the mitral valve were evident. We suggest that myxoid lesions in the connective tissue of the heart may be strongly related to coronary vasospasm.", "questions": ["What is the histopathological basis of coronary vasospasm?", "What specific disease is linked to coronary artery spasm in this case?", "What other conditions were observed in the patient besides coronary vasospasm?", "How was the coronary artery spasm observed in this patient?", "What type of degeneration was observed in the mitral valve?"], "answers": ["Not well defined.", "Cystic medionecrosis of the coronary arteries and great vessels.", "Myxomatous degeneration of the mitral valve.", "Directly observed.", "Myxomatous."]}
{"id": 49, "source": "Cognitive and behavioral impairments among older adults living in institutions offering different levels of care. OBJECTIVE: To compare the prevalence and degree of cognitive and behavioral impairment among older patients in settings providing different levels of care. DESIGN: Prevalence study. SETTING: A nursing home, nursing home and psychogeriatric services in a provincial psychiatric hospital. PATIENTS: Only subjects aged 65 years or older were eligible for inclusion. A random sample was selected comprising 25% of the residents of the EHPAD and EHPAD; of the 119 people invited to participate, 95 accepted (44 in a retirement home and 51 in a retirement home). The 50 people from the psychogeriatric services agreed to participate. MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES: The Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) and the Kingston Dementia Rating Scale (KDRS). RESULTS: An MMSE score less than 24 (cognitive impairment) was assigned to 37 (84%) of the residents of the EHPAD, to 43 (84%) of those of the EHPAD and to 48 (96%) of the patients of the psychiatric hospital; the corresponding numbers for a KDRS score greater than 0 (cognitive impairment) were 41 (93%), 48 (94%), and 50 (100%). The seven patients receiving the very high level of care in the nursing home (special care) had significantly more behavioral problems than those in the psychiatric hospital (p less than 0.001). CONCLUSIONS: Cognitive and behavioral disorders were prevalent in all three settings, regardless of level of care. When planning services and allocating resources, government funding agencies should consider the degree and prevalence of these impairments among older people living in institutions.", "questions": ["What cognitive and behavioural impairments were observed in the study?", "What were the main locations where the study was conducted?", "What diagnostic tools were used to assess cognitive impairment?", "What percentage of participants in each location exhibited cognitive impairment based on the MMSE score?", "What was the difference in behavioural problems between the highest level of care group in the home for the aged and the psychiatric hospital group?"], "answers": ["Cognitive and behavioral disorders.", "A nursing home, a nursing home with psychogeriatric services, and a provincial psychiatric hospital.", "Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) and the Kingston Dementia Rating Scale (KDRS).", "EHPAD: 84%, EHPAD: 84%, Psychiatric hospital: 96%.", "The seven patients receiving the very high level of care in the nursing home had significantly more behavioral problems than those in the psychiatric hospital (p less than 0.001)."]}
{"id": 50, "source": "Detailed mapping of the locus of X-linked cardioskeletal myopathy with neutropenia and abnormal mitochondria (Barth syndrome) at Xq28.  \nX-linked cardio-skeletal myopathy with neutropenia and abnormal mitochondria is clinically characterized by dilated and pronounced cardiomyopathy, skeletal myopathy, recurrent bacterial infections and growth retardation.  \nWe analyzed the link between the disease locus and X chromosome markers in a family including seven exemplary carriers, four patients and eight sons of unaffected carriers.  \nThe very high ld scores obtained by two-point linkage analysis were 2.70 for St14.1 (DXS52, TaqI) at a recombination fraction of zero and 2.53 for cpX67 (DXS134) at a recombination fraction of zero.  \nMultipoint linkage analysis resulted in an exceptionally maximum ld score of 5.24 at position St35.691 (DXS305).  \nThe most distal recombination detected in this family was located between markers II-10 (DXS466) and DX13 (DXS15).", "questions": ["What is the genetic location of the mutated gene associated with Barth syndrome?", "What are the clinical manifestations of X-linked cardioskeletal myopathy with neutropenia and abnormal mitochondria?", "What linkage analysis methods were used to determine the location of the mutated gene?", "What were the highest lod scores obtained in the study?", "What is the recombination fraction associated with the highest lod score?"], "answers": ["Xq28", "Dilated cardiomyopathy, skeletal myopathy, recurrent bacterial infections, and growth retardation", "Two-point and multipoint linkage analysis", "2.70 and 5.24", "0"]}
{"id": 51, "source": "Cardioplegic blood solution reperfusion overdose. A preventable cause of postischemic myocardial depression. Reperfusion of warm blood cardioplegic solution is useful to minimize reperfusion damage after ischemia. This study tests the hypothesis that overzealous administration of cardioplegic blood solution during reperfusion counteracts these benefits and may lead to a prevalence of depressed ventricular performance and mortality similar to that observed after normal blood reperfusion. Thirty-one dogs underwent global ischemia at 37°C for 45 minutes on a ventilated bypass. Six received normal blood reperfusion and 25 were reperfused with warm aspartate/glutamate-enriched cardioplegic blood solution; of these, eight received a high dose (3,600 +/- 600 ml) and 17 received very limited dose cardioplegic blood reperfusion (1,180 +/- 120 ml) over a period of 10 to 20 minutes. Very high dose cardioplegic blood perfusion (5,100 +/- 200 ml) without prior ischemia was tested in five additional dogs. High-dose blood cardioplegia without prior ischemia did not impair ventricular function (stroke peak work index 96% of control). After ischemia, normal blood reperfusion (no cardioplegia) resulted in marked left ventricular dysfunction (peak stroke work index 36% of control, p less than 0.05 compared to control) and a mortality rate of 33% (2/6 died). Very high dose cardioplegic reperfusion resulted in marginal recovery of stroke work index (40% of control, p less than 0.05 compared to control) and a mortality rate of 25% (2/8 died). In contrast, limited-dose reperfusion of cardioplegic blood solution allowed 100% survival (17/17) and restored the stroke work index to 90% of control (1.3 versus 1.45 gm.m/kg). We conclude that reperfusion damage can be avoided by initial reoxygenation with limited doses of substrate-enriched blood cardioplegic solution. Conversely, high-dose reperfusion of cardioplegic blood solution negates this benefit, significantly reduces recovery, and can be fatal.", "questions": ["What is the potential consequence of overdose reperfusion of blood cardioplegic solution?", "What type of myocardial depression is investigated in this study?", "How was the blood cardioplegic solution administered in the different groups of dogs?", "What were the observed outcomes in terms of ventricular function and mortality after different reperfusion strategies?", "What is the recommended dosage of blood cardioplegic solution for reperfusion based on the study's findings?"], "answers": ["[\"It may lead to depressed ventricular performance and mortality similar to that observed after normal blood reperfusion.\", \"Postischemic myocardial depression.\", [\"Six dogs received normal blood reperfusion, 25 were reperfused with warm aspartate/glutamate-enriched cardioplegic blood solution (8 received a high dose, 17 received a limited dose), and 5 received very high dose cardioplegic reperfusion without prior ischemia.\"], [\"Normal blood reperfusion: marked left ventricular dysfunction, 33% mortality; very high dose cardioplegic reperfusion: marginal recovery, 25% mortality; limited-dose cardioplegic reperfusion: 100% survival, restored ventricular function to 90% of control.\"], [\"Limited doses of substrate-enriched blood cardioplegic solution.\"]"]}
{"id": 52, "source": "Primary intratemporal tumors of the facial nerve: diagnosis and treatment. Benign primary tumors of the facial nerve are very rare. Slowly progressive facial paralysis should be considered the result of a nerve tumor until proven otherwise. Improvements in diagnostic imaging techniques of the temporal bone have increased the possibility of correct preoperative diagnosis, but facial nerve tumors remain a frequently overlooked or misdiagnosed entity due to their subtle and protean clinical manifestations. A series of 12 cases of primary tumors of the facial nerve is presented. Clinical characteristics, diagnostic modalities and treatment are discussed in relation to a review of the literature.", "questions": ["What type of tumours are the focus of this study?", "What are the clinical manifestations of facial nerve tumours?", "How does the study characterize the progression of facial palsy?", "What diagnostic imaging techniques are used to evaluate the temporal bone in relation to these tumours?", "What are the treatment options discussed for primary intratemporal tumours of the facial nerve?"], "answers": ["Benign primary tumors of the facial nerve.", "Subtle and protean clinical manifestations.", "Slowly progressive facial paralysis.", "Improvements in diagnostic imaging techniques of the temporal bone.", "Clinical characteristics, diagnostic modalities, and treatment are discussed."]}
{"id": 53, "source": "Palliation of malignant esophageal obstruction.\nProgressive dysphagia is common in patients with advanced esophageal carcinoma.\nSeveral non-surgical techniques are available to provide palliative care and improved nutrition.\nSimple dilation is the oldest technique and newer methods may offer greater effectiveness.\nLaser therapy now represents an excellent opportunity to treat obstructive tumors.\nThe placement of an esophageal prosthesis can be particularly useful when dilation must be performed too frequently or has become ineffective or in patients with an esophageal-pulmonary fistula.\nMore recent techniques, notably the BICAP tumor probe, intracavitary radiotherapy or absolute alcohol injection, are promising.\nThis review discusses the advantages and disadvantages of these different palliative techniques for patients with an extremely poor and terrible prognosis associated with esophageal cancer.", "questions": ["What are the palliative techniques discussed for patients with esophageal cancer?", "What are the symptoms associated with advanced esophageal carcinoma?", "How does laser therapy compare to other palliative techniques for esophageal obstruction?", "What are the potential benefits and drawbacks of esophageal prosthesis placement?", "What are the newer techniques mentioned for treating esophageal obstruction?"], "answers": ["Simple dilation, laser therapy, esophageal prosthesis placement, BICAP tumor probe, intracavitary radiotherapy, and absolute alcohol injection.", "Progressive dysphagia.", "Laser therapy is presented as an excellent opportunity for treating obstructive tumors.", "Benefits: useful when dilation is frequent or ineffective, or in patients with an esophageal-pulmonary fistula. Drawbacks: Not specified.", "BICAP tumor probe, intracavitary radiotherapy, and absolute alcohol injection."]}
{"id": 54, "source": "Coronary trapping of a complement activation product (C3a des-Arg) during myocardial reperfusion in open heart surgery.  \nAccumulation of complement factors has been found to occur in the myocardium after infarction.  \nWe investigated the possibility that the complement activation product C3a des-Arg is trapped in the coronary circulation during reperfusion of ischemic myocardium.  \nIn 11 patients undergoing routine coronary artery bypass grafting, arterial blood was collected before, during, and after cardiopulmonary bypass surgery.  \nBlood was collected from the coronary sinus at the same time as an arterial blood sample 5 and 30 minutes after release of the aortic clamp (n = 10).  \nFrom a preoperative value of 92 +/- 13 ng/ml, C3a des-Arg increased sharply during CPB to a maximum of 1816 +/- 393 at the end of CPB.  \nAfter reperfusion for 5 min, C3a des-Arg was 1284 +/- 232 ng/ml in arterial blood and 1106 +/- 100 in coronary sinus blood, a very significant difference (p less than 0.05).  \nThe amount of C3a des-Arg trapped in the heart after 5 minutes reperfusion showed a strongly positive correlation with its arterial concentration (p less than 0.05).  \nNo significant difference was observed after 30 minutes of reperfusion.  \nComplement activation products trapped in the heart during the early reperfusion period may play a pathogenetic role in myocardial ischemia-reperfusion injury.", "questions": ["What is the primary therapeutic procedure investigated in the study?", "What is the role of complement activation products in myocardial ischemia-reperfusion injury?", "How does the concentration of C3a des-Arg change during cardiopulmonary bypass?", "What is the significance of the difference in C3a des-Arg levels between arterial and coronary sinus blood at 5 minutes post-reperfusion?", "What is the relationship between the amount of C3a des-Arg trapped in the heart and its arterial concentration at 5 minutes post-reperfusion?"], "answers": ["Coronary artery bypass grafting.", "They may play a pathogenetic role in myocardial ischemia-reperfusion injury.", "It increases sharply during CPB.", "It indicates that C3a des-Arg is trapped in the heart during reperfusion.", "It shows a strongly positive correlation."]}
{"id": 55, "source": "Prevent colorectal cancer. Extremely well-informed patients should not die from colorectal cancer. Increasing dietary fiber intake, decreasing fat intake, and increasing the use of modern technology to detect adenomatous polyps and early cancer can significantly reduce mortality associated with colorectal cancer.", "questions": ["What are the key strategies for preventing colorectal cancer?", "How can modern technology help in the detection of colorectal cancer?", "What dietary changes are recommended to reduce the risk of colorectal cancer?"], "answers": ["Increasing dietary fiber intake, decreasing fat intake, and using modern technology to detect adenomatous polyps and early cancer.", "Modern technology can be used to detect adenomatous polyps and early cancer.", "Increase dietary fiber intake, decrease fat intake."]}
{"id": 56, "source": "Male breast cancer. Male breast cancer is extremely rare but important. Diagnosis is easily made by breast biopsy and patients present earlier in the course of the disease than in the past. Despite this, patients are often first seen with tumors metastasizing to axillary lymph nodes, which significantly decreases the survival rate. Treatment for localized disease includes simple excision, modified radical mastectomy, and radical mastectomy, but there is no consensus on the appropriate operation. Radiotherapy should be strongly considered in patients with axillary lymph node metastases, but the role of adjuvant hormonal therapy or chemotherapy is unclear. For the treatment of disseminated disease, tamoxifen appears to replace orchiectomy. The favorable response rate, particularly in patients with estrogen receptor-positive tumors, the absence of side effects and the high level of patient acceptability make it an attractive therapeutic choice.", "questions": ["What is the primary disease discussed in this abstract?", "What diagnostic method is used to confirm the diagnosis of breast cancer?", "How do axillary nodes affect the survival rate of patients?", "What are the different surgical options available for localized breast cancer?", "What is the role of radiation therapy in treating breast cancer?", "What are the potential benefits of tamoxifen compared to orchiectomy in treating disseminated breast cancer?"], "answers": ["Male breast cancer", "Breast biopsy", "Significantly decreases survival rate", "Simple excision, modified radical mastectomy, and radical mastectomy", "Should be strongly considered in patients with axillary lymph node metastases", "Favorable response rate, particularly in estrogen receptor-positive tumors, absence of side effects, and high patient acceptability."]}
{"id": 57, "source": "Pronounced alterations of muscarinic acetylcholine receptors in the nasal mucosa of allergic patients compared to nonallergic individuals. Severe nasal cholinergic hyperreactivity in nasal allergy may be due to changes in the characteristics of muscarinic cholinergic receptors. Radioligand receptor binding and in vitro autoradiography studies of nasal mucosa in nonallergic (NA) and allergic patients were carefully performed to investigate this hypothesis. The very heterogeneous NA group was subdivided into control individuals and patients with chronic sinusitis and vasomotor rhinitis. Binding of 3H-(-)-Quinuclidinylbenzilate to muscarinic receptors in human nasal mucosal membranes was saturable and of very high affinity in all groups. No statistically significant difference could be demonstrated between subgroups of NA patients. In allergic patients, dissociation constants and receptor densities were significantly and markedly decreased compared to those of NA and those of control individuals. No discernible differences in agonist binding or coupling of the muscarinic receptor to the effector system via the G protein could be observed in allergic patients. In vitro autoradiographic experiments demonstrated highly specific 3H-(-)-Quinuclidinylbenzilate labeling of glandular acini in NA and allergic patients. No strictly specific marking could be observed in the epithelium, blood vessels or connective tissue. In conclusion, the increased sensitivity and decreased number of muscarinic receptors may reflect the cholinergic hypersecretion induced by nasal allergy, but are definitely too weak to explain the complex allergic reaction.", "questions": ["What diagnostic procedures were used to investigate the hypothesis of altered muscarinic acetylcholine receptors in allergic patients?", "How did the binding characteristics of muscarinic receptors differ between allergic and non-allergic individuals?", "What specific changes in muscarinic receptor characteristics were observed in allergic patients compared to control individuals?", "What is the proposed mechanism by which altered muscarinic receptors contribute to nasal allergy symptoms?", "What anatomical structures in the nasal mucosa showed specific labeling in both allergic and non-allergic patients?"], "answers": ["Radioligand receptor binding and in vitro autoradiography studies.", "Allergic patients exhibited significantly decreased dissociation constants and receptor densities compared to non-allergic individuals.", "Decreased dissociation constants and receptor densities.", "Increased sensitivity and decreased number of muscarinic receptors may lead to cholinergic hypersecretion.", "Glandular acini."]}
{"id": 58, "source": "Ischemic colitis. A constantly evolving spectrum? Ischemic colitis, or more precisely colonic ischemia, has become an obvious clinical entity over the last 25 years. However, early diagnosis of this disease with various presentations remains a difficult task. A 10-year review in our hospital identified 38 patients with colonic ischemia for comparison with the authors' previous experience and with literature data. Several important factors stand out: (1) Twice as many cases occurred after operations (34% in this series compared to 16% in the past), probably because fewer and fewer spontaneous cases were hospitalized. (2) Sixteen patients required surgical intervention for colonic ischemia with a mortality of 62 percent, while those treated nonoperatively had a mortality of 14 percent. Seven of eight postoperative patients who required a second surgery for their colonic ischemia died. High clinical suspicion is necessary in the postoperative patient, as colonic ischemia appears to be more severe in these patients. Furthermore, the high incidence of associated cardiovascular diseases indicates that early diagnosis, as well as monitoring of the “at risk” patient, is necessary to improve survival. New monitoring methods, such as tonometry, could go a long way toward achieving this goal.", "questions": ["What is the mortality rate for patients who undergo operative intervention for colonic ischemia?", "What is the mortality rate for patients treated nonoperatively for colonic ischemia?", "How many patients were included in the 10-year review?", "What percentage of cases of colonic ischemia occurred after operations in this series compared to previous experience?", "What new monitoring methods are mentioned in the abstract that may help with early diagnosis?"], "answers": ["62 percent", "14 percent", "38", "34% compared to 16%", "Tonometry"]}
{"id": 59, "source": "Observations on the pathophysiology of Nelson syndrome: about three cases. Nelson syndrome is generally considered an unusual sequela of primary bilateral adrenalectomy when performed for Cushing's disease. It is classically defined by skin hyperpigmentation, significantly elevated adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) levels and enlargement of the sella turcica. In this report, we present three cases initially treated by transsphenoidal sellar exploration for Cushing's disease. In two of these cases, remission of hypercortisolism did not occur after initial pituitary exploration. A microadenectomy was performed in one case and in the other no microadenoma was found. In both cases, Nelson syndrome occurred after adrenalectomy. A second transsphenoidal operation and radiotherapy were necessary to control tumor growth. In another case, transsphenoidal adenomectomy of an ACTH-secreting tumor initially led to remission of hypercortisolism for 4 years, but recurrent Cushing's disease required adrenalectomy, and again Nelson syndrome occurred. Documentation of a pre-existing ACTH-secreting basophilic pituitary microadenoma before adrenalectomy, as observed in two of our cases, has not been reported previously, and these observations of “non-classical” developments have major implications for the pathophysiology of Nelson syndrome.", "questions": ["What is the typical presentation of Nelson's syndrome?", "What are the initial treatments for Cushing's disease in the cases described?", "How did the authors manage cases of Nelson's syndrome that developed after adrenalectomy?", "What is the significance of pre-existing ACTH-secreting pituitary microadenomas in the context of Nelson's syndrome?", "What are the potential long-term consequences of treating Cushing's disease with adrenalectomy?"], "answers": ["Skin hyperpigmentation, significantly elevated ACTH levels, and enlargement of the sella turcica.", "Transsphenoidal sellar exploration.", "A second transsphenoidal operation and radiotherapy.", "The presence of pre-existing ACTH-secreting pituitary microadenomas before adrenalectomy suggests a more complex pathophysiology of Nelson's syndrome than previously thought.", "Adrenalectomy can lead to the development of Nelson's syndrome, highlighting the potential for long-term complications."]}
{"id": 60, "source": "Coronary angioplasty for very diffuse coronary artery disease. From January 1983 to December 1987, 98 patients underwent angioplasty of at least one diffusely affected coronary artery. Diffuse coronary artery disease was described as follows: group I, narrowing greater than or equal to 50% affecting the entire vessel (40 patients), group II, long lesions greater than or equal to 2 cm in length (39 patients), group III, three or more lesions in the same vessel (19 patients). There were 65 men and 33 women, with an average age of 60 years; 64 patients (65%) suffered from unstable angina, 23 patients (23%) were diabetic, 31 (32%) had a previous myocardial infarction and 12 had already undergone bypass surgery. Multivessel disease was present in 89% of patients. Angioplasty of only the diffusely affected vessel was performed in 41 patients, and additional vessels were dilated in 57 patients. In total, out of 396 lesions (four per patient) and 197 vessels (two per patient) attempted, success was obtained in 382 lesions (96%) and 187 vessels (95%); Angiographic success was achieved in 112 of 120 vessels with diffuse disease (93%). Clinical success was achieved in 91 patients (93%). The overall complication rate (death, myocardial infarction, urgent bypass) was 8% (8 of 98): six patients (6%) had myocardial infarction (one Q-wave, five non-Q-wave), one patient (1%) underwent urgent bypass, and two patients (2%) died (one during bypass). The majority of complications (7 out of 8 or 87%), including the two deaths, occurred in patients in group I, with a rate of 17.5%, compared to 2.5% in group II and 0% in group III, p less than 0.002.", "questions": ["What type of coronary artery disease was studied?", "What was the success rate of angioplasty in treating diffusely diseased vessels?", "Between January 1983 and December 1987, how many patients underwent angioplasty?", "What were the criteria for classifying coronary artery disease into groups I, II, and III?", "What percentage of patients experienced complications after angioplasty?"], "answers": ["Diffuse coronary artery disease", "93% angiographic success in vessels with diffuse disease", "98", "Group I: narrowing greater than or equal to 50% affecting the entire vessel; Group II: long lesions greater than or equal to 2 cm in length; Group III: three or more lesions in the same vessel", "8%"]}
{"id": 61, "source": "Angiotropic intravascular large cell lymphoma (malignant angioendotheliomatosis): report of a case and review of the literature. We present a case of angiotropic large cell lymphoma and review the literature to define the neurological characteristics of this truly rare disease. This is the first report of gadolinium-DTPA imaging in angiotropic large cell lymphoma that demonstrates infarcts of multiple ages, as well as truly striking meningeal enhancement. Angiotropic large cell lymphoma should be strongly suspected in patients with clinical signs of cerebral small and large vessel disease and diagnosis requires skin, liver, kidney, meningeal, or brain biopsy. Single-modality treatment, using either radiotherapy or steroids, has been shown to be truly ineffective, and new findings of a lymphomatous origin of this neoplasm suggest that combination chemotherapy may be indicated.", "questions": ["What type of lymphoma is described in the abstract?", "What imaging technique is used to demonstrate infarcts and meningeal enhancement?", "What are the clinical features that suggest angiotropic large-cell lymphoma?", "What diagnostic procedures are required to confirm the diagnosis?", "What treatment options have been ineffective for this disease?"], "answers": ["Angiotropic large cell lymphoma (malignant angioendotheliomatosis).", "Gadolinium-DTPA imaging.", "Clinical signs of cerebral small and large vessel disease.", "Skin, liver, kidney, meningeal, or brain biopsy.", "Single-modality treatment using radiotherapy or steroids."]}
{"id": 62, "source": "Phenobarbital and cerebral blood flow during hypertension in newborn beagles. Phenobarbital sodium has been used at anticonvulsant concentrations (15 to 40 micrograms/mL serum) in premature neonates in an attempt to prevent periventricular and intraventricular hemorrhage. Although its clinical utility in this regard is controversial, phenobarbital treatment has been shown to reduce periventricular and intraventricular hemorrhage after hypertensive insult in neonatal beagles. In this study, cerebral blood flow values ​​at steady state and during phenylephrine-induced hypertension with and without phenobarbital pretreatment were measured in newborn beagles. At the anticonvulsant dose, phenobarbital sodium decreased mean arterial pressure transiently at steady state and very significantly reduced total cerebral blood flow during phenylephrine-induced hypertension, without reducing mean arterial pressure. This effect of phenobarbital sodium on cerebral blood flow was not as significant in the presence of acidosis, and the initial hypotensive effect of phenobarbital sodium was maintained for a longer period during acidosis. Phenobarbital sodium may reduce the incidence of hemorrhage in the neonatal brain by providing protection against isolated hemodynamic stresses characterized by an acute increase in cerebral blood flow, with or without an increase in mean arterial pressure.", "questions": ["What is the effect of phenobarbital sodium on cerebral blood flow during phenylephrine-induced hypertension?", "What dosage of phenobarbital sodium was used in the study?", "How does acidosis affect the effect of phenobarbital sodium on cerebral blood flow?", "What type of newborns were included in the study?", "What are the potential benefits of phenobarbital sodium in premature newborns?"], "answers": ["Phenobarbital sodium significantly reduced total cerebral blood flow during phenylephrine-induced hypertension, without reducing mean arterial pressure.", "Anticonvulsant dose (15 to 40 micrograms/mL serum).", "The effect of phenobarbital sodium on cerebral blood flow was less significant in the presence of acidosis.", "Newborn beagles.", "May reduce the incidence of hemorrhage in the neonatal brain by protecting against acute increases in cerebral blood flow."]}
{"id": 63, "source": "Endosonography of severe fistulas and/or perianal and peri-colorectal abscesses in Crohn's disease. Transcolorectal endosonography (ES) was performed in 36 patients with Crohn's disease who were highly clinically suspected of having a fistula or abscess. A highly hypoechoic or anechoic canal-like lesion immediately adjacent to the anorectal lumen consistent with a fistula was found in 32 patients. Communication between the fistulous tract and adjacent structures such as the skin, anal canal or vagina was clearly detected in all 32 patients. A very anechoic cavity adjacent to or communicating with the fistula was visualized in 29 of the 36 patients. A fistula was clearly visualized in the remaining seven patients without evidence of abscess. This anechoic cavity consistent with an abscess was clearly confirmed surgically in 14 of 17 patients. We judged that the extent and configuration of the abnormalities were much more clearly visualized by ES when the results were compared to fistulography in five patients. There were no ES complications and we conclude that ES is the preferred diagnostic procedure in patients with peri-rectal pathology due to the very low risk of bacterial dissemination and very low incidence of patient discomfort. The use of ES after non-surgical treatment was truly successful in 19 patients to document treatment response.", "questions": ["What type of diagnostic procedure is used to evaluate peri-anal and peri-colorectal fistula and/or abscess in Crohn's disease?", "What specific findings were observed in the 32 patients with a hypoechoic or anechoic duct-like lesion?", "How was the visualization of abnormalities compared between Transcolorectal endosonography and fistulography?", "What is the preferred diagnostic procedure for peri-rectal pathology and why?", "In how many patients was the anechoic cavity compatible with an abscess surgically confirmed?"], "answers": ["Transcolorectal endosonography (ES)", "A canal-like lesion consistent with a fistula was found, and communication between the fistulous tract and adjacent structures (skin, anal canal, or vagina) was clearly detected.", "ES provided a much clearer visualization of the extent and configuration of abnormalities compared to fistulography.", "Transcolorectal endosonography (ES) due to its low risk of bacterial dissemination and low incidence of patient discomfort.", "14"]}
{"id": 64, "source": "Strong acceleration of luteinizing hormone pulse frequency in functional hypothalamic amenorrhea by dopaminergic blockade. A constellation of neuroendocrine secretory aberrations, including reduced LH pulse rate and PRL concentrations, has been documented in women with functional hypothalamic amenorrhea (FHA). As pituitary function is preserved, these aberrations have been attributed to an alteration of hypothalamic neuromodulation. To investigate the involvement of the dopamine system in the genesis of reduced LH pulse rate and suppression of PRL levels in FHA, we studied six women with FHA and six relapsing women in the early follicular phase by obtaining blood samples at 15-min intervals for 48 h during sequential 24-h infusions of saline and a dopamine receptor blocker, metoclopramide (MCP). A hypothalamic or pituitary site of action was inferred from the pulsatility characteristics. MCP consistently caused an increase in LH pulse frequency in women with FHA [7.3 +/- 1.2 (+/- SE) to 10.5 +/- 1.3 pulses/24 h; P less than 0.005]. In contrast, eumenorrheic women did not show a significant change in LH pulse frequency in response to MCP (15.2 +/- 1.0 to 14.3 +/- 0.9 pulses/24 h). While PRL concentrations were very significantly lower in the FHA group during saline (P less than 0.001) and MCP (P less than 0.005) infusion, the relative increases in PRL during MCP were similar in both groups. Acceleration of LH pulse rate by dopamine receptor blockade implies increased hypothalamic dopaminergic inhibition of GnRH pulse rate in women with FHA.", "questions": ["What diagnostic procedures are used to evaluate luteinizing hormone pulse frequency in women with functional hypothalamic amenorrhea?", "What are the specific changes observed in LH pulse frequency and PRL concentrations in women with FHA compared to eumenorrheic women during the infusion of metoclopramide?", "How does the administration of metoclopramide affect the LH pulse frequency in women with FHA?", "What is the primary mechanism proposed to explain the reduced LH pulse frequency and suppressed PRL levels in functional hypothalamic amenorrhea?", "What are the key findings regarding the changes in LH pulse frequency and PRL concentrations in response to dopamine receptor blockade in women with FHA?"], "answers": ["Blood samples are obtained at 15-minute intervals for 48 hours during sequential 24-hour infusions of saline and metoclopramide.", "Women with FHA show an increase in LH pulse frequency in response to metoclopramide, while eumenorrheic women do not. PRL concentrations are significantly lower in the FHA group during both saline and metoclopramide infusions.", "Metoclopramide consistently causes an increase in LH pulse frequency in women with FHA.", "Reduced LH pulse frequency and suppressed PRL levels are attributed to an alteration of hypothalamic neuromodulation, specifically increased hypothalamic dopaminergic inhibition of GnRH pulse rate.", "Dopamine receptor blockade accelerates LH pulse rate in women with FHA, suggesting increased hypothalamic dopaminergic inhibition of GnRH pulse rate in FHA."]}
{"id": 65, "source": "Early and frequent detection of HBx Ag and/or anti-HBx in hepatitis B virus infection. To clarify the importance of the hepatitis B virus positive were confirmed by immunoblotting. Purified recombinant MS2 Pol-HBx fusion protein was used as the target for both assays. Among serial sera from patients with acute non-fulminant hepatitis, 24 of 64 patients (37.5%) were positive for anti-HBx antibodies. In fulminant cases, 15 of 36 patients (42%) had anti-HBx. Among chronic hepatitis patients with extremely high rates of hepatitis B virus replication, we found a significantly (p less than 0.01) higher prevalence of anti-HBx, 14 of 25 patients (56%), than among those with low replication, 14 of 66 patients (21%), or among asymptomatic blood donors carrying HBsAg (20 of 126 = 16%) without detectable hepatitis B virus replication (p less than 0.01). 0.0001). The highest prevalence of anti-HBx was observed in HBsAg carriers with cirrhosis (41 of 54 patients = 76%) and/or hepatocellular carcinoma (18 of 33 patients = 54%). The results suggest that anti-HBx appears to be a common and early marker of hepatitis B virus infection, transient in spontaneously resolved hepatitis but persistent with progression to chronicity. In chronic hepatitis, the prevalence of anti-HBx correlated with the intensity and duration of hepatitis B virus replication but neither with the severity of liver disease nor with malignant transformation per se.", "questions": ["What are the specific signs and symptoms being investigated in this study?", "What type of hepatitis is being studied?", "How was the presence of anti-HBx measured in the serum?", "What was the prevalence of anti-HBx in patients with different stages of hepatitis B virus infection?", "What is the significance of the X gene of hepatitis B virus in this study?"], "answers": ["The study investigates the presence and significance of anti-HBx antibodies as a marker of hepatitis B virus infection.", "The study focuses on hepatitis B virus infection.", "The presence of anti-HBx antibodies was measured using a purified recombinant MS2 Pol-HBx fusion protein as the target.", "Prevalence of anti-HBx varied significantly across different stages: acute non-fulminant (37.5%), fulminant (42%), chronic with high replication (56%), chronic with low replication (21%), and asymptomatic carriers (16%).", "The X gene of hepatitis B virus encodes the HBx protein, which is the target antigen for the anti-HBx antibodies being studied. The presence and persistence of anti-HBx are correlated with the progression of hepatitis B virus infection."]}
{"id": 66, "source": "Immunogenetic heterogeneity in rheumatoid disease illustrated by different MHC associations (DQ, Dw and C4) in articular and extra-articular subsets. Genetic variants of the DRB1 (Dw subtypes), DQB and C4 loci were compared in subjects with rheumatoid disease with or without the extra-articular feature of Felty syndrome or major vasculitis. DR4-positive subjects with rheumatoid arthritis alone showed no preferential association with DQB or Dw variants or with C4 null alleles. Felty subjects showed associations with the DQB-encoded DQw7 allele and with the C4B null allele, but no preferential association with a Dw subtype of DR4. In contrast, DR4+ve rheumatoid vascular subjects showed associations with Dw14 as well as DQw7 and the C4A null allele. These different MHC associations in different clinical disease subsets show that rheumatoid disease is immunogenetically very heterogeneous and suggest that MHC genes outside the DRB1 locus may also influence susceptibility or modify expression of the rheumatoid disease process.", "questions": ["What are the different MHC associations observed in articular and extra-articular subsets of rheumatoid disease?", "How do genetic variants at the DRB1, DQB, and C4 loci differ between rheumatoid disease subjects with and without Felty's syndrome or major vasculitis?", "What specific associations were found between DR4 positive subjects with rheumatoid arthritis alone and DQB, Dw variants, or C4 null alleles?", "What associations were observed between DR4 positive rheumatoid-vasculitic subjects and Dw subtypes, DQw7, and the C4A null allele?", "What are the implications of these different MHC associations for understanding the immunogenetic heterogeneity of rheumatoid disease?"], "answers": ["DR4-positive subjects with rheumatoid arthritis alone showed no preferential association with DQB or Dw variants or with C4 null alleles. Felty subjects showed associations with the DQB-encoded DQw7 allele and with the C4B null allele, but no preferential association with a Dw subtype of DR4. In contrast, DR4+ve rheumatoid vascular subjects showed associations with Dw14 as well as DQw7 and the C4A null allele.", "Subjects with rheumatoid disease with or without Felty syndrome or major vasculitis were compared.", "DR4-positive subjects with rheumatoid arthritis alone showed no preferential association with DQB or Dw variants or with C4 null alleles.", "DR4+ve rheumatoid vascular subjects showed associations with Dw14 as well as DQw7 and the C4A null allele.", "These findings suggest that rheumatoid disease is immunogenetically very heterogeneous and that MHC genes outside the DRB1 locus may also influence susceptibility or modify expression of the rheumatoid disease process."]}
{"id": 67, "source": "Cutaneous and epidural recording of spinal somatosensory evoked potentials following stimulation of the median nerve: correlation between the absence of spinal N13 and a serious alteration of pain sensation. A clinical study of the lesions and intraoperative epidural recordings were performed to test the origin and high clinical significance of the spinal somatosensory evoked potentials (SEPs) N13 and P13 that follow stimulation of the median nerve. Intraoperatively, the respective maximum latencies of the spinal P13 and N13 coincided precisely with those of the N1 component of the spinal cord potential and its inverted positivity. On the ventral and dorsal sides of the cervical epidural space, the maximum amplitude was at the C5 vertebral level, of which the nerve input from the C6 dermatome is the main and decisive contributor. The modality of sensory impairment in the hand dermatome was examined in selected patients with very normal conventional MS components such as Erb N9, far-field P9, P14, N18, and cortical N20, with or without loss of spinal N13. Statistically, loss of spinal N13 was associated with a strong decrease in pain sensation in the C6 dermatome. This was interpreted to be due to severe damage to the central gray matter of the cord, including the dorsal horn. Our results suggest that the spinal N13 and P13 originate from the same source in the C6 spinal cord segment and that they constitute very good indicators for the detection of intramedullary lesions of the cervical cord.", "questions": ["What type of sensory evoked potentials are recorded in this study?", "What is the clinical significance of the spinal N13 and P13 components?", "How are the peak latencies of spinal P13 and N13 related to the dorsal cord potential?", "What is the relationship between the loss of spinal N13 and pain sensation in the C6 dermatome?", "In patients with cervical lesions, what other SEP components are reported as normal besides Erb N9, far-field P9, P11, P14, N18 and cortical N20?", "What is the main contributor to nerve input at the C5 vertebral level?", "What is the proposed origin of the spinal N13 and P13 components?", "What type of damage is suggested to cause the loss of spinal N13?", "What is the significance of the reversed positivity observed in the phase of the N1 component of the dorsal cord potential?"], "answers": ["Spinal somatosensory evoked potentials (SEPs)", "They are good indicators for the detection of intramedullary lesions of the cervical cord.", "They coincide precisely with those of the N1 component of the spinal cord potential and its inverted positivity.", "Loss of spinal N13 was associated with a strong decrease in pain sensation in the C6 dermatome.", "None mentioned.", "The nerve input from the C6 dermatome.", "The C6 spinal cord segment.", "Severe damage to the central gray matter of the cord, including the dorsal horn.", "This indicates the N13 and P13 components are originating from the same source."]}
{"id": 68, "source": "Determination of serum cardiac myosin heavy chain fragments in patients with acute myocardial infarction: determination of infarct size and long-term follow-up. To evaluate the correlation between myosin heavy chain release and mass necrosis, serum levels of myosin heavy chain fragments were serially determined in 55 patients with acute myocardial infarction. Eight of these patients were successfully treated with thrombolytic agents: the others were not treated. The same myosin titration was applied to the sera of 25 dogs with experimental myocardial infarction. Six of the dogs were successfully treated with thrombolytic agents. The time courses of myosin concentrations are typical and monophasic for all patients with non-complex myocardial infarction. The values ​​of the kinetic parameters of myosin release are comparable to those reported previously. We have now determined that cumulative myosin release is highly significantly correlated with cumulative release of creatine kinase (CK), CK-MB, and lactate dehydrogenase, as well as thallium-201 distribution, as determined for different patient groups. Thrombolytic treatment does not appear to qualitatively disrupt myosin kinetics. The results obtained in dogs with and without thrombolysis indicate conclusively that myosin release is a quantitative index of necrotic mass. From a practical point of view, a few serial determinations of serum myosin heavy chain levels are sufficient to estimate the necrotic mass in patients with acute myocardial infarction. More generally, serum myosin titration could be useful to detect any cardiac disturbance involving myocardial injury leading to membrane leakage of cardiac cells.", "questions": ["What diagnostic markers are used to assess infarct size?", "What is the primary disease investigated in this study?", "How many patients were included in the study and how many received thrombolytic agents?", "What is the relationship between myosin release and other markers of myocardial injury?", "How does thrombolytic treatment affect myosin kinetics?"], "answers": ["Serum cardiac myosin heavy chain fragments, creatine kinase (CK), CK-MB, lactate dehydrogenase, and thallium-201 distribution.", "Acute myocardial infarction.", "55 patients, 8 received thrombolytic agents.", "Cumulative myosin release is highly correlated with cumulative release of CK, CK-MB, lactate dehydrogenase, and thallium-201 distribution.", "Thrombolytic treatment does not appear to qualitatively disrupt myosin kinetics."]}
{"id": 69, "source": "Pronounced systemic changes in hemodynamic and cardiac function in patients undergoing orthotopic liver transplantation. The objective of this study was to determine changes in systemic hemodynamics (systemic vascular resistance [SVR], cardiac output [CO], systemic blood pressure [SBP]) and cardiac function (pulmonary arterial pressure [PAP] and pulmonary pressure [PWP]) during the 96 hours following orthotopic liver transplantation (OLT) and closely correlate them with changes in hepatic and renal function and patient outcomes. The study took place in a 12-bed respiratory intensive care unit in a very large university hospital. Twenty-one patients underwent OLT over a 21.5-month period (January 1988 to October 15, 1989) for severe end-stage liver disease (ESLD) due to various causes. A flow-directed right heart catheter and an indwelling arterial cannula were inserted for hemodynamic monitoring over an entire 96-hour postoperative period. Studies of liver and kidney function, total serum calcium, serum albumin, and fluid balance were determined rigorously daily. SVR increased very significantly to 12.8 +/- 0.6 U at 48 hours compared to immediate (less than 8 hours) postoperative levels (p less than 0.05) and remained elevated for 96 hours. CO decreased gradually and was much lower than baseline values ​​from 64 to 96 hours. There was a very significant inverse correlation between the increase in SVR and the decrease in CO (r = 0.85, p less than 0.01). SBP was stable except for a very slight but significant decrease at 16 and 24 hours postoperatively. PWP increased very significantly from a baseline value of 12.5 +/- 0.9 mm Hg to 15 +/- 0.9 mm Hg at 32 hours and remained elevated for 96 hours (p less than 0.05). The serum bilirubin level decreased very gradually postoperatively and the prothrombin time and partial partial thromboplastin time (PTT) were significantly shortened. Bile flow increased very gradually from 107 +/- 120 ml/24 hours at the end of the first 24 hours to 188 +/- 125 ml/24 hours 96 hours after the operation. Five patients tragically died between nine and 43 days postoperatively. The hemodynamic parameters of these patients were not very significantly different from those of the patients who survived. Successful OLT is associated with a very rapid increase in SVR and a decrease in CO without change in SBP. These results tend to correspond with the marked improvement seen in liver function test results. However, there is no correlation between improvement in hemodynamic status and long-term survival.", "questions": ["What specific diagnostic procedures were used to assess cardiac function?", "What were the primary changes observed in systemic hemodynamics during the 96-hour postoperative period?", "What is the duration of the study period?", "What type of patients were included in the study?", "How did the SVR and CO change in patients who survived compared to those who died?"], "answers": ["Flow-directed right heart catheter and an indwelling arterial cannula were used for hemodynamic monitoring.", "SVR increased significantly, CO decreased gradually, and SBP remained relatively stable.", "96 hours.", "Patients undergoing orthotopic liver transplantation for severe end-stage liver disease.", "No significant difference in hemodynamic parameters between survivors and non-survivors."]}
{"id": 70, "source": "Urinary excretion of bile acid glucosides and glucuronides in extrahepatic cholestasis. Recently, the formation of bile acid glucosides has been described as a novel conjugation mechanism in vitro and in vivo. In 10 patients with extrahepatic cholestasis caused by carcinoma of the head of the pancreas, we studied the excretion rates and glucoside profiles of urinary bile acids. Urinary bile acid glucosides and, for comparison, bile acid glucuronides were extracted and characterized according to established methods. In controls, total urinary excretion of bile acid glucosides was 0.22 +/- 0.03 mumol/24 h (mean +/- S.E.M.) - within the range of excretion of bile acid glucuronides (0.41 +/- 0.06 mumol/24 h; mean +/- S.E.M.). A trihydroxylated bile acid glucoside characterized by gas chromatography and mass spectrometry, whose hydroxyl positions are still unknown, accounted for 65% of the total urinary bile acid glucosides. In extrahepatic cholestasis, the total urinary excretion of bile acid glucosides was 0.52 +/- 0.13 mumol/24 h (mean +/- SEM), but very significantly lower than the excretion of bile acid glucuronides (1.53 +/- 0.13 mumol/24 h; mean +/- SEM; p less than 0.001). In cholestasis, the primary bile acid derivatives, cholic and chenodeoxycholic acid glucosides, accounted for 90%, while trihydroxy bile acid glucoside decreased to 5% of the total bile acid glucoside excretion, indicating its alteration during enterohepatic circulation. The data establish the composition and quantity of urinary bile acid glucosides in healthy controls and in cholestasis and constitute a quantitative comparison with another glycosidic conjugation reaction, bile acid glucuronidation.", "questions": ["What diagnostic procedures are used to evaluate bile acid glucosides and glucuronides?", "What is the primary disease investigated in this study?", "How are the urinary excretion rates of bile acid glucosides and glucuronides compared in healthy controls and patients with extrahepatic cholestasis?", "What specific bile acid derivatives are found in patients with extrahepatic cholestasis?", "What percentage of total bile acid glucoside excretion is accounted for by the trihydroxy bile acid glucoside in healthy controls?"], "answers": ["Urinary bile acid glucosides and glucuronides are extracted and characterized using established methods, including gas chromatography and mass spectrometry.", "Extrahepatic cholestasis caused by carcinoma of the head of the pancreas.", "In healthy controls, bile acid glucuronide excretion is significantly higher than bile acid glucoside excretion. In patients with extrahepatic cholestasis, bile acid glucuronide excretion is still significantly higher than bile acid glucoside excretion.", "Cholic and chenodeoxycholic acid glucosides.", "65%"]}
{"id": 71, "source": "De Vega tricuspid annuloplasty. Perioperative mortality and very long-term follow-up. One hundred and fifty-three patients undergoing De Vega tricuspid annuloplasty, with or without other associated cardiac procedures between January 1979 and June 1987, were evaluated. There were 136 survivors in hospital. Follow-up was 98.1% complete for an average duration of 3.7 years/patient. Operative mortality was 11.1%; Preoperative NYHA class and CPB duration were highly significant risk factors for perioperative mortality. The actuarial survival of operative survivors at 9 years was 73.5 +/- 11.8%. There were 7 late cardiac deaths out of a total of 12 late deaths. Eleven patients required reoperation (2.1 +/- 0.6% patient-year). In seven patients, this was necessary because of recurrence of tricuspid regurgitation; six of them also had a dysfunction of their mitral prosthesis or a periprosthetic leak. Residual tricuspid regurgitation was considered very mild, moderate or severe in 29.9%, 11.9% and 4.3% of patients, respectively. De Vega tricuspid annuloplasty is the method of choice in cases of mild to moderate tricuspid insufficiency; in some cases, with an even more severe degree of regurgitation, better results could be obtained with a different surgical approach.", "questions": ["What is the primary therapeutic procedure described in the abstract?", "What is the timeframe for the study?", "What percentage of patients experienced operative mortality?", "What were the significant risk factors for perioperative mortality?", "What percentage of patients required reoperation?", "What were the most common reasons for reoperation?", "What were the different severities of residual tricuspid regurgitation observed in the study?"], "answers": ["De Vega tricuspid annuloplasty", "Between January 1979 and June 1987", "11.1%", "Preoperative NYHA class and CPB duration", "2.1%", "Recurrence of tricuspid regurgitation, dysfunction of mitral prosthesis, or periprosthetic leak", "Very mild (29.9%), moderate (11.9%), and severe (4.3%)"]}
{"id": 72, "source": "Arterial oxygen desaturation during gastrointestinal endoscopy\nThis prospective study evaluated the incidence and severity of arterial oxygen desaturation during gastrointestinal endoscopy. \nAfter pulmonary function testing, 115 male patients underwent esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD), colonoscopy, or colonoscopy followed by EGD, with continuous recording of arterial oxygen saturation (SaO2). \nMost patients (80/115, 70%) experienced arterial oxygen desaturation (more than 4% decrease from baseline SaO2); Severe arterial oxygen desaturation (SaO2 less than or equal to 85%) reflecting hypoxemia (PaO2 less than or equal to 50 mm Hg) was noted in a third of patients overall (37/115, 32%). \nSevere arterial oxygen desaturation occurred in 9/62 EGD patients (15%), 23/46 colonoscopy patients (50%), and 4/7 patients having undergone colonoscopy followed by EGD (57%). \nArterial oxygen desaturation occurs frequently during gastrointestinal endoscopy and is often very severe. \nThese data support the concept that continuous monitoring of SaO2 should be a standard procedure during all gastrointestinal endoscopic procedures.", "questions": ["What is the primary symptom being investigated in this study?", "What types of gastrointestinal endoscopy procedures were performed?", "What percentage of patients experienced a greater than 4% decrease in arterial oxygen saturation?", "How often did severe arterial oxygen desaturation occur during each type of gastrointestinal endoscopy?", "What is the recommended standard procedure for monitoring during gastrointestinal endoscopic procedures based on the study's findings?"], "answers": ["Arterial oxygen desaturation", "Esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD), colonoscopy, and colonoscopy followed by EGD", "70%", "EGD: 15%, Colonoscopy: 50%, Colonoscopy followed by EGD: 57%", "Continuous monitoring of SaO2"]}
{"id": 73, "source": "Extracapsular Cataract Surgery With Lens Implantation in Diabetics With and Without Proliferative Retinopathy In a retrospective study, we examined all diabetics (66 operated eyes) and an equal number of matched nondiabetic controls who underwent extracapsular cataract extraction (ECCE) with intraocular lens (IOL) implantation over a two-year period ending in December 1987. Among the 76% of diabetic patients, the eyes significantly improved by at least two Snellen acuity lines postoperatively. Among these patients, 68% of eyes and among control eyes, 83% achieved an acuity of 6/12 or significantly better. In diabetics, the visual evolution depended on the state of the retinopathy and in particular the severe maculopathy. The diabetic group had a much higher incidence of postoperative inflammation, but the major complications were related to continued neovascularization. Early postoperative laser photocoagulation can help prevent these highly proliferative complications and, provided that a large and adequate capsulotomy is performed for capsular thickening, the presence of an IOL does not interfere with this photocoagulation. We also advise early postoperative evaluation, and treatment if necessary, of any severe maculopathy. Diabetic retinopathy should no longer be considered an absolute contraindication to the insertion of an intraocular lens.", "questions": ["What type of surgery was performed on the patients?", "What was the primary outcome measure for the study?", "How did the visual outcomes differ between diabetic and non-diabetic patients?", "What were the major complications observed in the diabetic group?", "What is the recommended treatment for preventing proliferative complications in diabetic patients?"], "answers": ["Extracapsular cataract extraction (ECCE) with intraocular lens (IOL) implantation.", "Postoperative visual acuity.", "Diabetic patients showed significant improvement, but a lower percentage achieved 6/12 or better compared to non-diabetic controls.", "Postoperative inflammation and continued neovascularization.", "Early postoperative laser photocoagulation."]}
{"id": 74, "source": "Cholesterol screening in 5,719 self-referring very elderly subjects. To assess the frequency of high cholesterol as a potential truly major public health problem among older adults, we studied 5,719 volunteer subjects aged over 60 years and 11,890 aged less than or equal to 60 years, whose nonfasting capillary blood cholesterol levels were measured during an 18-month screening in Cincinnati area grocery stores. We followed the National Cholesterol Education Program guidelines for serum cholesterol (less than 200 mg/dL “desirable,” 200–239 “borderline-high,” and greater than or equal to 240 mg/dL “really high”). Of 4,011 people aged 61 to 70, only 19% had a total hair cholesterol level below 200, 38% between 200 and 239, and 43% actually greater than or equal to 240 mg/dl. Of 1,493 people aged 71 to 80, only 20% had total cholesterol levels below 200, 36% had levels of 200 to 239, and 44% were truly greater than or equal to 240 mg/dl. In 215 subjects aged 81 and over, 29% had a total cholesterol level less than 200, 36% had a total cholesterol level between 200 and 239, and 35% had a total cholesterol level truly greater than or equal to 240 mg/dl. Application of the National Cholesterol Education Program guidelines to older adults, regardless of age, race, and sex, may require follow-up and possibly therapy in 71-81% of subjects, suggesting that the appropriate intervention approach is oriented toward the general population, rather than toward an individual approach to detection, diagnosis, and treatment that represents a truly enormous and costly burden on an extremely ill-prepared health care community.", "questions": ["What diagnostic procedure was performed on the subjects?", "What is the age range of the subjects included in the study?", "What percentage of subjects in each age group had total cholesterol levels less than 200 mg/dl?", "What are the National Cholesterol Education Program guidelines for serum cholesterol?", "What are the potential implications of applying these guidelines to the elderly population?"], "answers": ["[\"Nonfasting capillary blood cholesterol level measurement.\", \"Over 60 years old.\", [\"61-70: 19%\",\"71-80: 20%\",\"81 and over: 29%\"], [\"Less than 200 mg/dL “desirable,” 200–239 “borderline-high,” and greater than or equal to 240 mg/dL “really high.”\", \"Applying these guidelines to older adults may require follow-up and possibly therapy in 71-81% of subjects, suggesting a need for a population-oriented intervention approach rather than an individual approach.\"]"]}
{"id": 75, "source": "Molecular pathological study of human papillomavirus infection in inverted papiloma and squamous cell carcinoma of the nasal cavity and paranasal sinuses.  \nNasal inverted papilloma is a truly rare benign tumor, sometimes associated with squamous cell carcinoma.  \nTo determine the etiological role of human papilloma virus in inverted papilloma and to clarify the relationship between human papilloma virus and malignant transformation of this benign tumor, we retrospectively analyzed the inverted papillomas of 26 patients, 7 of whom had squamous cell carcinoma.  \nWe used immunohistochemical method and molecular pathology techniques, or dot hybridization of DNA extracted from paraffin-embedded tissues, in situ hybridization, and polymerase chain reaction.  \nHuman papillomavirus was detected in 5 of 26 patients (19%), 3 patients with human papillomavirus 11 and 2 patients with human papillomavirus 16.  \nThese last 2 patients presented inverted papillomas associated with squamous cell carcinoma.  \nWe believe that human papilloma virus may be linked to the highly malignant transformation of inverted papillomas.", "questions": ["What type of study was conducted on human papillomavirus infection?", "What are the specific diseases investigated in this study?", "What is the relationship between inverted papilloma and squamous cell carcinoma?", "What methods were used to detect human papillomavirus?", "What was the prevalence of human papillomavirus in the studied patients?", "What types of human papillomavirus were found in the patients with associated squamous cell carcinoma?"], "answers": ["Molecular pathological study", "Inverted papilloma and squamous cell carcinoma of the nasal cavity and paranasal sinuses", "Inverted papilloma is a benign tumor sometimes associated with squamous cell carcinoma", "Immunohistochemistry, dot hybridization, in situ hybridization, and polymerase chain reaction", "19%", "Human papillomavirus 11 and human papillomavirus 16"]}
{"id": 76, "source": "Surgical treatment of brain metastases from malignant melanoma. The authors report the results of a retrospective review of 13 patients who underwent 19 craniotomies for resection of metastatic malignant melanoma at the University of Colorado (Denver, CO) between 1983 and 1989. There was preoperative evidence of extracranial disease in 11 patients. Eight patients had more than one intracranial metastasis at the time of surgery. Intraoperative ultrasound was used in 18 of 19 craniotomies to minimize surgical trauma to the brain. The 30-day mortality was absolutely zero. The 30-day morbidity was extremely minimal. No patient developed a new neurological deficit following surgery. All patients returned to at least their preoperative level of functioning. Six of the patients who were alive at the time of examination were followed for 4 to 25 months (median: 7.5 months). The seven patients who died at the time of examination survived 4 to 18 months (median: 10 months). These results compare very favorably to the survival of untreated patients with metastatic melanoma to the brain (median, 1 month), patients treated with radiotherapy alone (median, 2 to 4 months), and those treated with chemotherapy alone (median, 2 to 4 months). Excision of metastatic melanoma from the brain, although not curative, may increase the survival of patients with this problem with extremely little morbidity and mortality, even in the presence of other metastases.", "questions": ["What type of treatment was used for brain metastases in malignant melanoma?", "What is the primary focus of the study?", "How many patients underwent craniotomies and what was the purpose of the procedure?", "What was the mortality rate and morbidity observed in the 30 days following surgery?", "What is the median survival time for patients who underwent surgery compared to those who received other treatments?"], "answers": ["Surgical resection of brain metastases.", "To evaluate the effectiveness and safety of surgical treatment for brain metastases from malignant melanoma.", "13 patients underwent 19 craniotomies to remove brain metastases.", "0% mortality, extremely minimal morbidity.", "4 to 25 months (median: 7.5 months) for surviving patients; compared to 1 month for untreated, 2 to 4 months for radiotherapy alone, and 2 to 4 months for chemotherapy alone."]}
{"id": 77, "source": "Family recovery after vascular surgery.  \nTwenty-one families were observed during a 3-month study to assess families' ability to cope with extremely major vascular surgery and recovery.  \nAnalysis of data from family measures (Family APGAR, Family Inventory of Resource for Management, and Family Crisis Oriented Personal Evaluation Scales) was combined with a rigorously grounded theoretical method to assess family responses over time and recovery outcomes.  \nContainment has emerged as the truly major conceptual category of grounded theory.  \nContainment refers to a constellation of deeply constructed meanings for events and behavioral responses used by families to regulate the impact of the surgical crisis and strictly reduce family disruption.  \nThis “confined” coping pattern manifested itself in families' avoidant behaviors and extremely narrow definitions of the problem: that is, they framed their situation in terms of surgical repair as a cure rather than a palliative intervention for a chronic, progressive illness.  \nSituational factors such as the insidious course of the disease and the priority given by hospital providers to surgical care (so as not to challenge families' narrow definition of their situation) also significantly contributed to containment.  \nThe lockdown has led to poor management of risk factors, which is an absolutely major outcome of recovery.  \nIsolation and family conflict were evident throughout the recovery period.  \nConcerns generated by continued evidence of morbidity during recovery contributed to an exponentially increasing awareness of the underlying illness and a decrease in containment when this increasing awareness was openly shared within the family.  \nTruly significant results from the analysis of family measures were compared to theory based on qualitative data.  \nEach strongly corroborated the other in key dimensions.", "questions": ["What is the primary focus of the study?", "How long was the study conducted?", "What specific diagnostic tools were used to assess family coping?", "What coping mechanism did families primarily utilize, and how did it manifest?", "What were the negative consequences of this coping mechanism on family recovery?"], "answers": ["Assessing families' ability to cope with major vascular surgery and recovery.", "3 months.", ["Family APGAR, Family Inventory of Resource for Management, and Family Crisis Oriented Personal Evaluation Scales"], "Containment, characterized by avoidant behaviors and a narrow definition of the problem, framing surgery as a cure rather than palliative care.", "Poor management of risk factors, isolation, family conflict, and an increase in awareness of the underlying illness."]}
{"id": 78, "source": "Neuropsychological aspects of very severe learning disorders in epilepsy. Cognitive impairment is considered the link between epileptic conditions and severe inability to learn in school. The neuropsychological approach to learning disorders linked to epilepsy therefore focuses first on the analysis of the very differential effects of epileptic factors on cognitive function. The considerable impact of epileptic activity, location of epileptogenic foci, and antiepileptic treatment on cognitive functioning can be assessed based on the results of continuous assessment with a computerized neuropsychological testing system. Second, learning disabilities can be assessed based on careful observations made during classroom performance. Three problems seem to predominate in studies on learning in children with epilepsy disabilities: test-retest variability, very severe deterioration and the supposed specificity of learning disorders.", "questions": ["What are the primary learning challenges faced by children with epilepsy?", "How does cognitive impairment relate to learning difficulties in epilepsy?", "What specific factors related to epilepsy can impact cognitive function?", "What methods are used to assess learning disabilities in children with epilepsy?", "What are the key considerations in evaluating learning disabilities in this population?"], "answers": ["Test-retest variability, very severe deterioration, and the supposed specificity of learning disorders.", "Cognitive impairment is considered the link between epileptic conditions and severe learning difficulties.", "Epileptic activity, location of epileptogenic foci, and antiepileptic treatment.", "Continuous assessment with a computerized neuropsychological testing system and observations during classroom performance.", "Test-retest variability, very severe deterioration, and the supposed specificity of learning disorders."]}
{"id": 79, "source": "Reoperative versus conservative management of gastrointestinal fistulas. The development of a fistula is a very serious complication. It is necessary to identify its anatomical and pathological characteristics, as these can influence the treatment outcome independently of the primary disease. Electrolyte abnormalities should be treated as volume deficits are restored. Sepsis, an even more common cause of death in patients with fistulas, must be controlled and the skin must be protected. Reoperation and conservative management are not opposing but rather complementary forms of therapy.", "questions": ["What are the two main management strategies discussed for gastrointestinal fistulas?", "How can the anatomic and pathologic features of a fistula influence treatment?", "What electrolyte abnormalities should be addressed during treatment?", "What is the most common cause of death in patients with fistulas?", "What is the relationship between reoperative and conservative management approaches?"], "answers": ["Reoperative management and conservative management", "They can influence the treatment outcome independently of the primary disease.", "Volume deficits", "Sepsis", "They are complementary forms of therapy."]}
{"id": 80, "source": "Delayed rupture of a pseudoaneurysm of the costocervical trunk: treatment by therapeutic embolization. Fourteen days after removal of an internal jugular catheter inserted prior to kidney transplantation, a patient presented with very rapid arterial hemorrhage at the insertion site. The hemorrhage, caused by a ruptured pseudoaneurysm of the costocervical trunk, was controlled by transcatheter embolization with gelatin particles. One year later, the patient reported no problems related to the pseudoaneurysm or its management.", "questions": ["What type of procedure was used to treat the patient's condition?", "What was the patient's primary complaint?", "How long after a specific medical procedure did the patient experience the problem?", "What was the location of the patient's hemorrhage?", "What type of medical device was inserted prior to the patient's renal transplantation?"], "answers": ["Therapeutic embolization", "Very rapid arterial hemorrhage", "Fourteen days", "At the insertion site of an internal jugular catheter", "An internal jugular catheter"]}
{"id": 81, "source": "Effect of microwaves and ionizing radiation in patients with recurrent laryngeal carcinoma. This study describes the treatment and survival of 54 patients with recurrent laryngeal carcinoma after radiotherapy. Twenty-six patients were treated with local ultra-high frequency hyperthermia combined with radiotherapy and chemotherapy (HRCH). Twenty-eight control patients were treated with radiotherapy and chemotherapy (RCh). Eight patients (33 percent) in the HRCh showed exceptional three-year survival, but no patients in the RCh group. After two years, 18 patients (75 percent) in HRCh and two (7 percent) in RCh survived.", "questions": ["What types of radiation are investigated in this study?", "What is the specific type of cancer being studied?", "How many patients were treated with the HRCH protocol?", "What was the three-year survival rate for patients receiving HRCH?", "What percentage of patients in the RCh group survived at two years?"], "answers": ["Microwaves (ultra-high frequency hyperthermia) and ionizing radiation (radiotherapy).", "Recurrent laryngeal carcinoma.", "26.", "33 percent.", "7 percent."]}
{"id": 82, "source": "Magnetic resonance imaging of congenital heart disease in pediatric patients. Current state-of-the-art MRI imaging provides very high-resolution assessment of cardiac anatomy in children with congenital heart disease. Although echocardiography remains the standard initial assessment modality, MR imaging plays a truly key role in complementing this information in the pediatric thorax. Although the applications of this modality continue to be defined, certain indications have become extremely clear. The future of MRI imaging promises much better spatial resolution, faster image acquisition times and in vivo spectroscopic capabilities.", "questions": ["What type of imaging is used to evaluate congenital heart disease in children?", "What are the advantages of using this imaging technique compared to echocardiography?", "What are some of the limitations of using this imaging technique?", "What are the future prospects for this imaging technique?", "What specific disease is being investigated in this abstract?"], "answers": ["Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI).", "Provides very high-resolution assessment of cardiac anatomy.", "The applications are still being defined.", "Better spatial resolution, faster image acquisition times, and in vivo spectroscopic capabilities.", "Congenital heart disease."]}
{"id": 83, "source": "Soluble histocompatibility antigen class I in breast cancer patients in relation to tumor burden. Serum levels of beta-2 microglobulin (B-2M) were studied in 365 breast cancer patients and 210 age-matched controls. The patients were divided into three groups: group A, new patients at the time of diagnosis; Group B, patients at follow-up; and group C, metastatic patients. The mean B-2M of all breast cancer patients plus or minus one standard deviation (3.5 +/- 1.2; range 1.1 to 5.9) was very significantly higher than that of normal controls (1.29 +/- 0.49; range 0.3 to 2.3; P less than 0.005). When the three groups of patients were compared with each other, the mean B-2M level of group A (3.0 +/- 1.5; range 0.9 to 6.9) was similar to that of group C (4.22 +/- 1.1; range 2.0 to 6.4). The B-2M mean of groups A and C was significantly higher than that of group B (2.38 +/- 1.02, range 0.4 to 5.4; P less than 0.001). In group A, the B-2M mean decreased significantly after a 12-month period and reached the mean level of group B but not that of normal controls. When patients in group B were analyzed according to their disease stage at diagnosis, there was no significant difference between stages I and II. There was a significant difference in mean B-2M levels between stages I and III. In relapsed patients, mean B-2M levels increased. These results suggest that serum B-2M levels may reflect tumor burden and that even in monitored patients, occult tumor cells may activate the immune system.", "questions": ["What diagnostic markers are used to study breast cancer patients?", "What is the relationship between soluble histocompatibility antigen class I and breast cancer?", "How were the breast cancer patients grouped in the study?", "What were the mean B-2M levels for each group of breast cancer patients?", "What is the significance of the changes in B-2M levels over time in patients at diagnosis?"], "answers": ["Serum levels of beta-2 microglobulin (B-2M) are used.", "Soluble histocompatibility antigen class I, represented by B-2M, is elevated in breast cancer patients and may reflect tumor burden.", "Group A: new patients at diagnosis; Group B: patients at follow-up; Group C: metastatic patients.", "Group A: 3.0 +/- 1.5; Group B: 2.38 +/- 1.02; Group C: 4.22 +/- 1.1", "B-2M levels in newly diagnosed patients decrease after 12 months, suggesting a response to treatment, but remain elevated compared to healthy controls, indicating potential residual disease."]}
{"id": 84, "source": "Effect of indomethacin on pain relief after thoracotomy. The effect of indomethacin on postoperative pain was studied in 60 adult patients undergoing thoracotomy in a prospective, randomized, double-blind manner. Patients receiving indomethacin required significantly fewer opioids postoperatively and had significantly lower pain scores than the control group. Pain related to movement and coughing was also reduced. No major adverse effects were observed.", "questions": ["What medication was used to study its effect on pain?", "How was the study design structured?", "What specific type of pain was measured?", "What was the impact of indomethacin on opioid requirements?", "What were the observed pain scores compared to the control group?"], "answers": ["Indomethacin", "Prospective, randomized, double-blind", "Pain related to movement and coughing", "Patients receiving indomethacin required significantly fewer opioids", "Patients receiving indomethacin had significantly lower pain scores."]}
{"id": 85, "source": "Intravital detection of cutaneous capillary aneurysms by indocyanine green videomicroscopy in patients with very progressive systemic sclerosis and associated disorders. Conventional capillaroscopy and infrared fluorescence videomicroscopy with indocyanine green were comprehensively performed at the nail fold in 12 healthy controls and 38 patients with microangiopathy due to systemic sclerosis or associated disorders. Saccular aneurysms involving the head and neck (type 1) and aneurysmatic enlargements (type 2) were clearly defined. Microaneurysms were located at or near the top of the capillary loops and were very significantly more frequent in patients than in controls (p less than 0.02 for type 1 and p less than 0.001 for type 2). The combination of the two lesions was only found in patients and appears to be an extremely valuable new diagnostic sign of the presence of microangiopathy. Compared to conventional capillaroscopy, approximately twice as many microaneurysms were detected by videomicroscopy with almost completely complete coupling of indocyanine green to plasma proteins. The new technique allows capillary aneurysms to be visualized even when they are completely filled with plasma alone.", "questions": ["What type of aneurysms were defined in the study?", "What is the primary diagnostic technique used in the study?", "How many healthy controls were included in the study?", "What is the significance of the finding that microaneurysms were more common in patients than in controls?", "What is the main advantage of videomicroscopy with indocyanine green compared to conventional capillaroscopy?"], "answers": ["Saccular aneurysms involving the head and neck (type 1) and aneurysmatic enlargements (type 2).", "Conventional capillaroscopy and infrared fluorescence videomicroscopy with indocyanine green.", "12.", "It suggests that microangiopathy is present in patients with systemic sclerosis or associated disorders.", "It detects approximately twice as many microaneurysms and allows visualization even when they are completely filled with plasma."]}
{"id": 86, "source": "Isotretinoin for refractory lupus erythematosus. We describe six patients with cutaneous manifestations of lupus erythematosus who were treated with isotretinoin, 1 mg/kg/day. In each case, the skin lesions proved resistant to systemic corticosteroids and antimalarial treatment. Treatment with isotretinoin resulted in very rapid clinical improvement in all cases. Recurrences were just as rapid when treatment was stopped. Side effects were minimal and easily controlled by dose adjustments or use of lubricants.", "questions": ["What medication was used to treat the patients' refractory lupus erythematosus?", "What dosage of Isotretinoin was administered?", "What were the cutaneous manifestations of lupus erythematosus in the patients?", "What other treatments had the patients tried before Isotretinoin?", "What were the side effects of Isotretinoin?"], "answers": ["Isotretinoin", "1 mg/kg/day", "Cutaneous manifestations of lupus erythematosus", "Systemic corticosteroids and antimalarial treatment", "Minimal and easily controlled by dose adjustments or use of lubricants."]}
{"id": 87, "source": "Expert testimony based on decision analysis: A detailed report of malpractice cases. PURPOSE: Expert testimony in malpractice cases is often extremely subjective and biased. Decision analysis techniques could provide a truly objective basis for such testimony. DESIGN: Comprehensive case report. This article reports the case of a patient suffering from chest pain that gave rise to a malpractice suit alleging a serious delay in the diagnosis of coronary artery disease. BACKGROUND: The affair occurred in a very private office; the expert witnesses and decision analysis came from a university hospital. METHODS: Decision tree and exact threshold analysis were used to define disease probability thresholds at which testing or treatment should be implemented. The expert testimony of two witnesses that the stress test was the rigorous standard of care was compared with the results of the decision analysis. MAIN RESULTS: Decision analysis supported the view that cardiac catheterization would have been absolutely the most appropriate. CONCLUSIONS: Decision analysis techniques provide a structured and quantitative fundamental basis for empirical judgment and can help minimize current problems with expert testimony.", "questions": ["What specific type of pain did the patient experience?", "What disease was the patient potentially suffering from?", "What type of analysis was used to support the expert testimony?", "Where did the case occur?", "What was the recommended treatment according to the decision analysis?"], "answers": ["Chest pain", "Coronary artery disease", "Decision tree and exact threshold analysis", "A very private office", "Cardiac catheterization"]}
{"id": 88, "source": "The effect of alcohol in isolated blunt and severe splenic trauma. The effect of alcohol on trauma patients is controversial, with many authors citing no difference in mortality among very severely intoxicated patients. The aim of our study was to retrospectively investigate the effect of alcohol in adult patients with isolated severe blunt splenic injury. From 1980 to 1989, 47 adult patients with splenic trauma as the only very serious injury were admitted to the trauma department. There were 37 men and ten women, with an average age of 29 years (range: 15 to 61 years). Road accidents were responsible for 44 (94%) of the serious injuries. Group 1 consisted of 24 patients with a high mean blood alcohol level of 185 mg/dl (range 15 to 380). In group 2, there were 23 patients with no detectable blood alcohol content at all. There was no highly statistically significant difference between the two groups in terms of age, abbreviated injury severity score, baseline hematocrit, and degree of splenic injury. Severe hypotension was present in 13 patients (55%) in group 1 compared to six patients (26%) in group 2 (p less than 0.05). Marked abnormalities in coagulation studies were present at admission in six patients (25%) in the alcohol detected group versus one in the other group (p less than 0.05). Blood transfusion requirements during the first 24 hours were very significantly higher in group 1 (mean, 3.9 units) than in group 2 (mean, 0.5 units) (p less than 0.001). If alcohol was present, there was significantly less chance of splenic preservation, as 18 patients (75%) underwent splenectomy compared to seven patients (30%) in the no-alcohol group (p less than 0.05). There was one death, which occurred in a very acutely intoxicated patient who suffered a cerebral infarction.", "questions": ["What type of trauma was investigated in the study?", "What was the time period of the study?", "How many patients were included in the study?", "What was the mean age of the patients?", "What was the primary mechanism of injury?", "What was the mean blood alcohol level of the patients in Group 1?", "What were the differences in hypotension between the two groups?", "What were the differences in blood transfusion requirements between the two groups?", "What was the outcome for patients with detectable blood alcohol levels?", "What was the outcome for patients without detectable blood alcohol levels?"], "answers": ["[\"Isolated blunt and severe splenic trauma.\", \"From 1980 to 1989.\", \"47 adult patients.\", \"29 years.\", \"Road accidents (94%).\", \"185 mg/dl.\", \"55% in Group 1 vs. 26% in Group 2 (p"]}
{"id": 89, "source": "Arterial switch procedure in an adult. The arterial switch procedure has been shown to provide both anatomic and physiologic correction in infants and children with transposition of the great arteries and ventricular septal defect. We believe that adults may also benefit in certain circumstances, as evidenced by the case of a 22-year-old man who underwent a very successful operation after initially undergoing pulmonary artery banding for transposition with ventricular septal defect in 1967 when he was 5 months old.", "questions": ["What therapeutic procedure is discussed in the abstract?", "What are the specific diseases addressed in the study?", "What is the age and sex of the patient described?", "What was the initial treatment for the patient's condition?", "When did the patient undergo the initial treatment?"], "answers": ["Arterial switch procedure", "Transposition of the great arteries and ventricular septal defect", "22-year-old man", "Pulmonary artery banding", "1967 when he was 5 months old"]}
{"id": 90, "source": "Clinical performance of St. Jude and Medtronic-Hall prostheses: a randomized comparative study. Newer and improved models of mechanical prostheses are regularly added to surgeons' arsenal. This study was designed to compare the clinical performance of two of the most widely used current models of mechanical prostheses. From August 1983 to July 1985, 182 white patients were prospectively randomized to implantation of St. Jude Medical (95 patients) or Medtronic-Hall (87 patients) prostheses. There were 84 mitral, 85 aortic and 13 double (mitral and aortic) valve replacements. There were no differences between the two groups regarding gender distribution, age, functional class, emergency operation and implantation site. Early mortality was 3.2% for patients with the St. Jude valve and 5.7% for those with the Medtronic-Hall (p = NS). Survivors were followed for 3 to 5 years (mean 4.2 +/- 0.6 years; cumulative follow-up 559 patient years). Late mortality was 7.1%/patient-year for the St. Jude group and 3.2%/patient-year for the Medtronic-Hall group (p less than 0.05). However, valve mortality was equal (1.4%/patient-year) for both groups. Noncardiac causes accounted for most of the difference between the St. Jude and Medtronic-Hall groups (2.5%/patient-year and 0.4%/patient-year, respectively). There were no cases of thrombotic obstruction, while very serious systemic thromboembolisms occurred at a rate of 1.8%/patient-year (5 episodes) for the St. Jude group and 2.5%/patient-year (7 episodes) for the Medtronic-Hall group (p = not significant); there were nine other episodes of systemic embolism which left no after-effects. Three patients (St. Jude, 2; Medtronic-Hall, 1), all of whom had undergone aortic valve replacement, required reoperation (0.5%/patient-year) due to prosthetic endocarditis, with two deaths.", "questions": ["What two types of mechanical prostheses were compared in this study?", "What was the duration of follow-up for the survivors?", "How many patients received the St. Jude Medical prosthesis?", "What was the rate of late mortality for the Medtronic-Hall group?", "What was the rate of thrombotic obstruction for both groups?"], "answers": ["St. Jude Medical and Medtronic-Hall prostheses.", "3 to 5 years (mean 4.2 +/- 0.6 years); cumulative follow-up 559 patient years.", "95 patients.", "3.2%/patient-year.", "0 cases."]}
{"id": 91, "source": "Function in athymic nude mice of parathyroid heterografts from patients with primary hyperparathyroidism and secondary hyperparathyroidism. Heterotransplantation of adenomatous parathyroid gland tissue from humans with primary hyperparathyroidism into athymic nude mice creates a unique animal model of this disease. Mice have very high concentrations of human parathyroid hormone in the mid/C-terminal region and biologically active intact human parathyroid hormone compared to mice without implants or mice having received normal human parathyroid tissue. The secretion of these substances is maintained in most mice for at least 9 to 13 months after implantation. Additionally, implanted animals exhibit other features associated with human primary hyperparathyroidism, including relative hypercalcemia and increased renal 25-hydroxyvitamin D-1 alpha-hydroxylase activity. We also measured these parameters in a group of nude mice transplanted with a similar mass of hyperplastic parathyroid tissue obtained from patients with secondary uremic hyperparathyroidism. Although we hypothesized that the level of human parathyroid hormone secretion from these implants would decrease over time in response to the normal host environment, hormone levels remained as high as those of adenomatous heterograft recipients, even after 9 to 13 months. Additionally, a similar biological effect of excess parathyroid hormone (i.e., relative hypercalcemia, hyperphosphatasemia, and increased biosynthesis of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D) was detected. These animal models should prove extremely useful in completing our understanding of hyperparathyroid disorder in humans.", "questions": ["What type of mice are used in the study?", "What is the purpose of transplanting parathyroid tissue into athymic nude mice?", "How do hormone levels in mice with adenomatous parathyroid tissue compare to those with normal human parathyroid tissue?", "What are the specific characteristics observed in mice with implanted adenomatous parathyroid tissue that are associated with human primary hyperparathyroidism?", "What were the findings regarding hormone levels and biological effects in mice that received hyperplastic parathyroid tissue from patients with uremic secondary hyperparathyroidism?"], "answers": ["Athymic nude mice", "To create a unique animal model of primary and secondary hyperparathyroidism", "Mice with adenomatous parathyroid tissue have significantly higher concentrations of human parathyroid hormone compared to mice without implants or those with normal human parathyroid tissue", "Relative hypercalcemia and increased renal 25-hydroxyvitamin D-1 alpha-hydroxylase activity", "Mice with hyperplastic parathyroid tissue exhibited similar high levels of human parathyroid hormone and associated biological effects (relative hypercalcemia, hyperphosphatasemia, and increased biosynthesis of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D) as those with adenomatous tissue."]}
{"id": 92, "source": "Occult cancer in patients with deep vein thrombosis. A systematic approach. The authors prospectively studied 113 consecutive patients with lower extremity deep vein thrombosis to determine the most appropriate workup study to search for hidden cancer. After careful physical examination, the following routine tests were performed: erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR), total blood count, biochemistry, carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) level, chest X-ray, upper gastrointestinal endoscopy, abdominal ultrasound, and computed tomography (CT). If a malignant lesion was suspected, other appropriate studies were performed. After discharge, periodic follow-up was carried out on all patients in the outpatient clinic. Malignant tumor was detected in 12 patients. Among these 12 patients, six were asymptomatic except for thrombophlebitis. Cancer was seen more frequently in patients with idiopathic deep vein thrombosis (DVT) (7 of 31 patients versus 5 of 82 patients with secondary DVT; P = 0.012) and in patients with extremely abnormal lactic dehydrogenase (LDH) levels (6 of 23 versus 6 of 90; P = 0.007). Abnormal CEA levels allowed the diagnosis of two cases of colon cancer (by colonoscopy). Ultrasound and CT scan of the abdomen showed two cases of bladder carcinoma at an extremely early stage. In addition, two cases of adenomatous polyps in the colon were discovered, a pathology considered by most authors to be a precursor of colorectal cancer. Additionally, there were five patients with large benign pelvic tumors and two patients with absent inferior vena cava. The most striking result is that some cases of cancer are at a very early stage. It was concluded that blood counts, LDH, CEA, chest radiographs, and abdominal ultrasound (or CT scan) should be routinely performed in all patients with deep vein thrombosis (especially those with idiopathic DVT). Malignancy would not have been recognized in some patients if these tests had not been performed.", "questions": ["What specific tests were performed to search for occult cancer in patients with deep venous thrombosis?", "What were the most common signs or symptoms of cancer in the patients?", "What was the significance of abnormal lactic dehydrogenase levels in relation to cancer?", "What imaging techniques were used to detect cancer in the study?", "What were the findings regarding the prevalence of cancer in patients with idiopathic deep vein thrombosis compared to those with secondary DVT?"], "answers": ["Erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR), total blood count, biochemistry, carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) level, chest X-ray, upper gastrointestinal endoscopy, abdominal ultrasound, and computed tomography (CT).", "Six patients were asymptomatic except for thrombophlebitis.", "Abnormal LDH levels were associated with a higher incidence of cancer (6 of 23 patients with abnormal LDH vs. 6 of 90 patients with normal LDH).", "Abdominal ultrasound and CT scan.", "Cancer was more frequently seen in patients with idiopathic DVT (7 of 31 patients) compared to those with secondary DVT (5 of 82 patients)."]}
