 to a residual stenosis of 30% with the use of a 1.2 mm and then 1.8 mm diameter laser catheter. Early follow-up angiography, 24 hours later, revealed very persistent patency and unchanged lesion diameter of the target vessel. The patient was asymptomatic during the 2-month follow-up period, but died very suddenly while playing in a tennis tournament 63 days after the procedure. Postmortem histological examination revealed considerable restenosis of 80% at the lesion site without plaque rupture or thrombosis. Specific staining of the histological specimen for smooth muscle cells using alpha-actin revealed highly significant proliferation of smooth muscle cells at the site of excimer laser coronary angioplasty. However, a huge portion of the vessel narrowing appeared to be due to underlying fibrous plaque resulting from insufficient tissue ablation. This was likely related to the size of currently available catheters, which are extremely small to create a large channel.", "questions": ["What specific symptoms were experienced by the patient?", "What was the primary finding in the postmortem histologic examination?", "What type of tissue was removed during the coronary excimer laser angioplasty?", "What was the size of the laser catheter used in the procedure?", "What was the patient's age and sex?"], "answers": ["Exertional angina (Canadian Cardiovascular Society class III).", "Considerable restenosis of 80% at the lesion site without plaque rupture or thrombosis.", "Atherosclerotic intracoronary tissue.", "1.2 mm and then 1.8 mm diameter.", "53-year-old white man."]}
{"id": 99, "source": "Late effects of Wilms tumor treatment. A report from the National Wilms Tumor Study Group. The National Wilms' Tumor Study (NWTS) was initiated in 1969. One of its goals was to modulate treatments based on risk factors to minimize the number and severity of short- and long-term iatrogenic complications related to treatment. The NWTS therefore incorporated a long-term follow-up study (LTFS) into its framework to monitor late effects. The LTFS is limited to relapse-free survivors living 5 years or more after initial surgery, and data are collected using specially designed forms. A total of 787 patients enrolled in NWTS-1 or NWTS-2 (1969 to 1979) were eligible, of whom 680 (86%) were available for analysis regarding their musculoskeletal, cardiovascular, and neuropsychological status, as well as the presence of benign and malignant tumors. Patients with early-stage disease treated with radiation therapy experienced scoliosis, as well as other musculoskeletal abnormalities (32 versus 2), significantly more often than members of the cohort population who did not receive radiation therapy (35 of 57 versus 5 of 53, respectively). The difference in cardiovascular problems recorded among survivors who did or did not receive Adriamycin (Adria Laboratories, Columbus, OH) (2.4 versus 1.1 per 100 person-years at risk) was of borderline statistical significance (P = 0.06). No excess neuropsychological events were reported in people who received the neurotoxin vincristine. When considering patients with disease at all stages, the 5 second malignancies occurred in all 623 patients who underwent radiotherapy (RT patients); benign tumors were also more common in RT patients than in patients who did not receive radiotherapy (41 of 486 or 8% versus 4 of 194 or 2%). Continued study of this unique patient body is necessary, particularly for those receiving Adriamycin, due to the known long interval required for latent cardiomyopathy to manifest clinically in some patients.", "questions": ["What disease is the subject of this study?", "What was the primary objective of the National Wilms' Tumor Study?", "What specific types of health issues were monitored in the Long Term Follow-up Study?", "How did the incidence of scoliosis compare between patients who received radiation and those who did not?", "What was the observed difference in cardiovascular problems between patients who received Adriamycin and those who did not?"], "answers": ["Wilms tumor", "To minimize the number and severity of short- and long-term complications related to treatment by modulating treatments based on risk factors.", "Musculoskeletal, cardiovascular, and neuropsychological status, as well as the presence of benign and malignant tumors.", "Significantly higher in patients who received radiation therapy (32 versus 2) compared to those who did not (5 of 53).", "2.4 per 100 person-years at risk for those who received Adriamycin versus 1.1 per 100 person-years at risk for those who did not (borderline statistical significance, P = 0.06)."]}
{"id": 100, "source": "Diagnosis of very hairy oral leukoplakia by ultrastructural examination of exfoliative exfoliative cytological samples. Lingual exfoliative cytology samples (scrapings) were obtained from 18 human immunodeficiency virus-positive patients with very clinical oral hairy leukoplakia. Deep scrapings of the oral mucosa were obtained in 12 of these patients. The valuable specimens were processed for examination by transmission electron microscopy (TEM). Sixteen (89%) of the lingual samples revealed severe infection of keratinocytes with a herpes-like virus. There was no absolute evidence of viral infection in the 12 oral mucosal scrapings. Numerous fungal hyphae were observed by TEM in 14 (78%) of the lingual scrapings and two (17%) of the oral scrapings. One meticulous exfoliation sample and two biopsy samples were stained for Epstein-Barr virus DNA with a DNA probe. The detection of herpes-type virions by TEM in the keratinocytes of a lesion clinically highly suspected of being hairy leukoplakia allows a direct and objective diagnosis. Furthermore, the use of exfoliative cytological samples constitutes a clinically extremely simple and non-invasive technique.", "questions": ["What type of examination is used to diagnose oral hairy leukoplakia?", "What is the primary diagnostic procedure used in this study?", "What is the sample type used for the examination of oral hairy leukoplakia?", "How many patients with positive human immunodeficiency virus were included in the study?", "What percentage of lingual specimens showed infection of keratinocytes by herpes-type virus?"], "answers": ["Ultrastructural examination", "Transmission electron microscopy (TEM)", "Lingual exfoliative cytological samples (scrapings)", "18", "89%"]}
